{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh2ebiCPX5TkEHMKWKKJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TbQZHLyHmTWuApC1YvwMwp","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat >> PLAN.md <<'PLAN_EOF'\n\n---\n\n## 9. Presentation: art direction, assets, animation, effects\n\n### 9.1 Art direction\n\n- **The engine's native look is the base:** `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true`; HD Humanoid rigs at size 1.0–1.6, every\n  one dressed; textured TileMap floors and walls; `r.shape` props; canvas lighting on every scene (`enableGPU({ map })`\n  as well where WebGPU exists, with the same ambients); particles and `game.hitFx` on every hit; `E.font.title` logos;\n  `E.tones` shading on everything drawn by hand.\n- **On top of it, a retro-future TV studio:** neon, chrome, marquee bulbs, sponsor logos, confetti, spotlights, a\n  jumbotron. Check every screenshot against \"a Saturday-morning game show crossed with a PS1 arcade port\", never\n  against an NES game.\n- **Palette** (shade every colour with `E.tones`):\n\n  | Role | Colours |\n  |---|---|\n  | Shadow | `#140a24`, `#2a1446` |\n  | Neon | magenta `#ff2e88`, cyan `#2ee6ff` |\n  | Gold | `#ffcc33` |\n  | Chrome | `#c8d0e0` |\n  | Tally light | red `#ff3344` |\n  | Players | P1 `#e8443a`, P2 `#3b82f6`, P3 `#22c55e`, P4 `#f5b700` (the hub's seat colours, also used standalone) |\n  | Foe bullets | warm orange `#ff9a3c` and yellow cores, with dark outlines, never in player colours |\n\n- Information never rides on colour alone: players have numbers on their backs and HUD panels, and pickups carry letters.\n\n### 9.2 Sets (one per episode, plus the Green Room)\n\nEach set provides a floor texture function, a wall decorator, a door painter, an ambient and lights, and an **outside\ndressing** list. The outside dressing fills the space a wide or tall screen shows beyond the arena walls, in world\ncoordinates outside 0–368 × 0–240:\n- **Bleachers:** E and W, x −130 to −10 and 378 to 498. Stepped boxes with rows of audience sprites.\n- **North:** a lighting truss and the jumbotron at (184, −36, z 46), 120 × 64 units.\n- **South:** a camera dolly track with two camera cranes at (54, 272) and (314, 272), plus front-row audience.\n\n| Set | Floor | Walls and doors | Props in the arena (keep play space clear) | Outside | Light |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `studio` (Ep1, Lightning Round) | glossy black-purple tiles with magenta and gold inlay; the MASH TV logo painted in the centre (§9.4) | flats in purple velvet with gold trim and neon strips; marquee bulbs chasing along the top edge; shutter doors in gold frames | 4 spotlight stands in the corners; hazard stripes at doors | bleachers, audience, jumbotron, camera cranes, APPLAUSE and ON AIR signs | ambient 0.55; 4 coloured spotlights sweeping slowly; door beacons |\n| `warehouse` (Ep2) | sealed concrete with yellow safety lines and pallet scuffs | corrugated metal with MegaMart banners; roll-up loading-dock doors with red and green beacons | stacks of prize crates and gift boxes in the corners (asset store `kenney-holiday` presents, crates); conveyor belts in 2-2 | shelving racks of prizes (fridges, TVs and sofas from the store), a forklift, audience behind a chain-link barrier | ambient 0.6; cold fluorescent strips; amber beacons |\n| `rooftop` (Ep3) | dark slate tiles, a red carpet down the middle, a gold \"M\" helipad ring | glass balustrades with gold posts and velvet ropes; doors are elevator doors with brass frames | champagne towers, potted palms, a searchlight base in two corners | the city skyline glowing at the far edge (teal and amber window lights); searchlights; fireworks bursting in the sky every few seconds | ambient 0.4 (night); warm string lights; fireworks flashes; the searchlight cones |\n| `greenroom` | emerald carpet with a gold pattern | wood panelling with framed headshots of Lance | sofas, a fridge, a cake table, TVs (asset store) | — | ambient 0.6; warm lamps |\n\n### 9.3 Characters\n\n| Character | Build | Look |\n|---|---|---|\n| ROCKET (P1 default) | Humanoid `heroic`, size 1.1, `weapon: 'gun'` | red jumpsuit (outfit `shirt`, pants the same colour), white trim stripes, short hair, black boots and gloves, a white \"1\" on the back |\n| VOLT (P2) | as above | blue jumpsuit, `ponytail`, a goggles band (`hat: 'band'`), silver gloves, \"2\" |\n| MOXIE (P3) | as above | green jumpsuit, `spiky` hair, `cap`, \"3\" |\n| GOLDIE (P4) | as above | yellow jumpsuit, `long` hair, headband, \"4\" |\n| LANCE LUSTRE | `heroic`, size 1.3 | `spiky` silver pompadour, purple sequinned `coat` (gold trim; sparkle glints in draw), black pants, white gloves, a gold microphone drawn at `rig.hand()`. Portrait: an exaggerated grin with a tooth glint. |\n| PENNY PRIZE | `heroic`, size 1.2 | `long` pink hair, teal sequinned gown (`outfit: 'robe'`), white gloves, glitter particles |\n| Audience | small string sprites, 8 head-and-shoulders variants × `E.recolor` palettes | they bob, cheer (arms up), do the wave, and some hold signs (\"MASH!\", \"KA-CHING!\", \"HI MOM\") |\n| Paparazzi | Humanoid `chibi`, size 0.9 | trench coat, cap, a camera drawn at the hands (a flash on firing) |\n\n- **Contestants pick a look in the lobby;** the slot fixes the colour.\n- **In hub sessions** the look follows the seat, and the hub's names replace the flavour names.\n- **Enemy and boss looks** are in §5.8 and §5.9. Their build:\n  - **Humanoid** (outfits and palettes per kind): goon `bulky` 1.0; popper `chibi` 1.0 with a party hat; mime `heroic`\n    1.0 with white face paint, a striped shirt and a beret; heckler `chibi` 0.95 with a jersey and foam finger; brute\n    `bulky` 1.6 with a giant bear-mascot head and a cereal-box chest emblem.\n  - **Blob:** clapbot is a metallic `Blob` with drawn clapper hands and an antenna.\n  - **Custom painters** (`r.queue` + `E.px` polys shaded with `E.tones`): the disco ball (faceted sphere, rotating\n    glints), the dolly tank (box chassis, scrolling treads, camera-lens turret), the cable snake (striped cable discs and\n    a plug head) and every boss.\n\n### 9.4 New art assets to author (list them in `src/06-art-studio.js`, `07-art-chars.js` and `08-sprites.js`)\n\n**Floors** (floorTex functions):\n1. Studio gloss tiles.\n2. The **logo decal.** Rasterize \"MASH TV\" with `E.font.title` into an offscreen canvas once, then sample that canvas\n   inside the floorTex for the centre 160×60 area.\n3. Warehouse concrete with lines.\n4. Rooftop slate with the red carpet strip and helipad ring.\n5. Green Room carpet.\n\n**Walls and doors:**\n6. Wall decorators (`types[id].decorate(face)`): velvet flats with gold trim, corrugated metal with a banner, glass\n   balustrade, wood panelling.\n7. A door painter for 4 sides × 5 states. Shutter rise animation, beacons, and the exit sign with the destination text\n   and icon.\n8. Marquee-bulb chase strips (top of the walls, menu frames, the jumbotron border), drawn per frame as dots.\n\n**Studio dressing and props** (shape entries per `docs/ASSETS.md`, checked with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs`):\n9. Camera crane, studio camera on a tripod, spotlight stand, truss segment, jumbotron frame, APPLAUSE sign, ON AIR\n   sign, prize pedestal (turning top), prize cage, bleacher block, sponsor billboard, champagne tower, velvet rope\n   stanchion, searchlight, a vault safe with locks (3-4), and a forklift.\n10. Store props cut into one set with `tools/asset-set.mjs`: presents, crates, barrels, fridge, televisions, sofas,\n    cakes, speakers, lamps.\n\n**Characters and bosses:**\n11. Contestant, host and co-host rig definitions.\n12. Enemy painters and attachments: clapper hands, the popper's party popper, the mime's face paint, the brute's mascot\n    head, the tank, the disco ball, the snake.\n13. Boss painters: MASHY (body, arms, treads, hatch, eyes), THE WHEEL (8 labelled segments, bulbs, pointer, mount),\n    STARMAKER 9000 (podium, hover jets, turrets), THE NETWORK (a 4×3 TV wall and THE EYE).\n\n**Sprites** (string sprites, 8–16 px):\n14. Bullets for each weapon and foe kind; the rocket and popcorn bag; kernels; the gold record.\n15. Cash ×4, prize boxes ×3, key, capsule (a mini TV with icon slots), flashbulb, 1UP star, statuette, and the 18 prize\n    icons (12×12).\n16. HUD icons: weapons, hearts and HP pips, lives (mini contestant heads), keys, the room-type icons (sword, $, gun,\n    flame, star, skull), the LIVE dot, the MASH 9 bug.\n17. **Glyph icons** (§4.4): PlayStation, Xbox, keycaps, mouse.\n\n**Logos and decals:**\n18. The MASH TV logo: `E.font.title` \"MASH TV\" in a magenta→gold gradient with depth, inside a drawn CRT-TV outline\n    with bunny-ear antennas and a sparkle. Plus the \"LIVE FROM STUDIO 9\" sub-line.\n19. Decals: tomato splats, scorch marks, confetti on the floor that slowly fades.\n\n### 9.5 Animation (the engine's system as the base, plus new motion where the source needs it)\n\n**Contestants:** gait from velocity (automatic), and `point: true` with the facing on the aim when shooting.\n- The slide is a dive roll (`flip` 0→1).\n- Showboat cycles `cheer`, `hips` and `wave` with `expr: 'smile'`.\n- Hurt flinch (`hurt`), then death (`'die'`, then `down: 1`).\n- The respawn drop: a cable line from the top of the screen, a body descending, a land squash (`land: k`).\n- Victory `cheer`.\n- The walk-in at room start.\n\n**Host and co-host:** talking gestures, cycling `wave`, `hips` and `point` (the mic raised). On the jumbotron\nportraits, the mouth moves while text types (the Dialog portrait technique: `rig.drawPortrait`, with `expr` set while\ntalking).\n\n**Enemies:**\n- Goon: baton `E.MOVES.bash` with a 0.3 s wind; a stagger on hit.\n- Clapbot: hop squash and stretch (`blob.kick`), with hands clapping on a sine.\n- Popper: waddle, then a fuse swell (a scale tween and a red flash).\n- Mime: **\"trapped in a box\"**, a new animation. After `rig.update`, post-process the joints: hands flat at chest and\n  head height, sliding along invisible walls on a slow sine (wrap `rig.solvePose()` as `AI_GUIDE.md` describes). The\n  telegraph is a frantic shake.\n- Heckler: a leap arc, then the throw (`E.MOVES.throw`).\n- Brute: the stomp telegraph (`crouch`, then `kick` squash, rings), a charge lean (`sprint`), and a stunned wobble with\n  orbiting stars.\n- Tank: tread scroll speed from its velocity, turret slew, recoil on firing.\n- Snake: segments follow the head's path, with a sine weave.\n- Disco: facet glints rotate with its spin.\n\n**Bosses:** Mashy's arms aim and recoil, the treads scroll, the hatch tweens open, the googly eyes jiggle. The Wheel's\nspin uses angle easing (`outCubic`) down to the stop, with pointer clicks on each segment boundary. The Starmaker bobs,\nits jets flicker, and the mic drop is a rise-and-slam tween. The Network's screens flip channels with a static wipe, and\nTHE EYE dilates.\n\n**Studio:** audience bob amplitude grows with the ratings (the wave runs in Prime Time). Spotlights sweep. Marquee bulbs\nchase. The jumbotron has scanlines. Confetti cannons fire.\n\n**UI:** the logo drops in with `outBounce`. Menu focus has a pulsing bulb frame. Banners slide and bounce, and the\nnumbers count up. The \"TV static\" transition is 0.4 s of animated noise via `px.blend` plus a horizontal roll.\n\n**Check** every attack and pose with `node tools/filmstrip.mjs` (`--seed 1`): a wind-up, a strike and a clean settle.\n\n### 9.6 Effects, lighting, feel\n\n- **Every hit:** a hit flash and small sparks. **Every kill:** a confetti `particles.bits` in the enemy palette, a pop,\n  a cash pop-up (`particles.text`).\n  - Big kills (tank, brute, boss parts): `particles.explosion` + `game.shake(4)` + a `game.flash` for bosses.\n- **Hit-stop:** `game.freeze(.03)` on brute, tank and boss-part kills and on contestant hits. **Never in hub sessions**:\n  it would freeze the host's world for everyone.\n- **Lights:** each set's ambient, plus `game.lights.add` for spotlights, door beacons (red or green), bullet glows\n  (emissive), pyro flames, the laser and the jumbotron's glow. The Prime Time strobe follows the comfort setting.\n- **Comfort:** every shake, flash and strobe goes through `game.shake` and `game.flash`, which respect reduce motion,\n  screen shake and screen flashes.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Audio (everything synthesized in code)\n\n### 10.1 Music (engine notation songs in `src/03-audio-music.js`, all original)\n\n| id | Used for | Tempo and feel | Layers (`audio.layer`) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `theme` | title, Cold Open, episode cards | 132 bpm, a brassy game-show fanfare in C major, swing hi-hats | — |\n| `studioBattle` | Ep1 rooms, Lightning Round | 150 bpm driving funk: slap bass on triangle, square lead, drums | `prime` (brass stabs, `on: false`), `intense` (extra drums when 30+ enemies are alive) |\n| `warehouseFunk` | Ep2 rooms | 128 bpm disco funk, four-on-the-floor | `prime`, `intense` |\n| `finaleOverture` | Ep3 rooms | 160 bpm dramatic minor, saw strings | `prime`, `intense` |\n| `bossShowdown` | all bosses | 170 bpm, minor, relentless | `phase2` (brought in by later phases) |\n| `commercial` | Commercial Break, sponsor ads, Prize Recap intro | 112 bpm cheesy jingle | — |\n| `recap` | Prize Recap, Green Room | 100 bpm lounge (sine Rhodes, brushed drums) | — |\n| `ending` | endings and credits | 96 bpm, a warm reprise of the theme | — |\n\n- **Stings** (`audio.sting`): room clear \"ta-da\", prize fanfare, key chime, Prime Time fanfare, extra life, BANKRUPT\n  (descending trombone), Game Over (sad trombone), continue tick, \"MASH!\" start hit, boss-defeat fanfare.\n- A starting motif for the theme (develop it into 16+ bars; the whole score is original):\n  - lead: `G4 . C5 . E5 . G5 - - . E5 . G5 . A5 - | G5 - - - E5 - C5 - D5 - - - . . . .`\n  - bass: `C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 . A2 . A2 . F2 . G2 .`\n  - drums: `k . h . s . h . k k h . s . h o`\n- Use `music(song, { fade: .6 })` for crossfades. `audio.duck` comes from the engine during pause; duck the music\n  yourself under host lines too (−40 %).\n\n### 10.2 Sound effects (`audio.define` in `src/04-audio-sfx.js`)\n\n| Group | Sounds |\n|---|---|\n| Weapons | blaster pew, spread triple, rocket launch and boom, popcorn thunk and kernel pops, laser hum (re-triggered every 0.12 s while firing), records whirr, shield hum, sneakers squeak |\n| Contestants | slide whoosh, showboat flourish, Showstopper flashbulb pop + boom, hurt buzzer (\"wrong answer\"), death (descending \"wah\" + crowd \"ooh\"), respawn cable zip and land, cash ka-ching, prize fanfare, key chime, power-up rising arp, power-up expire click, extra life |\n| Enemies | goon grunt and baton whoosh, clapbot hop and clap, popper fuse hiss and pop, mime beep and pfft, disco shimmer and beam zap, heckler \"boo\" and tomato splat, tank lens whine and shell boom, snake hiss and segment snap, brute roar, stomp and wall crash, confetti pop on death |\n| Studio | door klaxon, door roll-up and slam, beacon tick, conveyor hum and reverse alarm, pyro whoosh, crowd ambience loop, applause, cheer, laugh, ooh, boo, drum roll, wheel click, TV static, channel flip, camera shutter |\n| UI | focus move (a soft blip), confirm (a bell), back, page flip, slider tick, typing blips (when no voice), countdown beeps, continue tick |\n\nUse the engine's voice objects (`wave`, `freq`, `to`, `dur`, `arp`, `vib`, filtered `noise`). Give each sound a\ndistinct contour, so you can tell weapons apart blind.\n\n### 10.3 Babble: Animalese/Simlish voices (`src/05-babble.js`)\n\nA small formant-synthesis voice engine on `game.audio.ctx`, routed through the engine's effects bus.\n- **Engine patch P4:** add a getter `game.audio.bus` that returns `_sfxBus` after `_init()`, so the voice follows the\n  volume and mute settings.\n\n**`MT.babble.say(text, voiceId, { speed, emotion })`:**\n- Schedules syllables from `ctx.currentTime` and returns the line's duration. Headless (no context), it returns the\n  duration and does nothing.\n- **Syllables:** split the text into words, and each word into syllables at vowel groups (cap 4 per word). Each\n  syllable's sounds come from a seeded hash of the word, so the same line always sounds the same.\n- **Each syllable:**\n  - An onset consonant by class: plosive (a 15 ms band-passed noise burst at 2–4 kHz), fricative (40–70 ms high-passed\n    noise), nasal (a 40 ms low sine) or none.\n  - A vowel nucleus, 60–120 ms at the voice's speed. The source is a sawtooth or pulse at `f0`, through 2 parallel\n    band-pass filters at the vowel's formants (a 800/1200, e 400/2300, i 300/2800, o 450/800, u 325/700; Q 8),\n    shifted by the voice's `formant` factor.\n  - A gain envelope: 5 ms attack, 30 ms release.\n- **Pitch:** `f0 = base × contour`.\n  - Words rise toward a `?`, fall at the end of a sentence, and jump +20 % for `!` or ALL-CAPS emphasis.\n  - Jitter ±4 % per syllable, plus a vibrato of 5.5 Hz at 2 %.\n  - Pauses: 80 ms at a comma, 160 ms at `.`, `!` or `?`, 300 ms at `...`.\n- **Voices:**\n\n  | id | base f0 | speed (syll/s) | wave | formant × | character |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|---|\n  | `lance` | 135 Hz | 13 | saw | 1.0 | fast, swooping, smarmy (big rises on \"!\") |\n  | `penny` | 300 Hz | 12 | pulse | 1.18 | bubbly, with giggle trills (arp at line ends) |\n  | `annc` | 85 Hz | 7 | saw | 0.85 | slow and booming, with a feedback-delay echo |\n  | `network` | 70 Hz | 8 | square | 0.8 | monotone, a waveshaper bitcrush, robotic |\n  | `mashy` | 110 Hz | 9 | triangle | 0.9 | goofy and bouncy |\n  | `contestant1–4` | 160–240 Hz | 12 | pulse | 1.05 | short exclamations: \"HUP!\", \"YES!\" |\n  | `goon` | 95 Hz | 10 | saw | 0.9 | grunts on spawn and hit, at most 2 at once |\n\n- **Subtitles:** the lower third types at a speed tied to the babble duration, so text and voice end together.\n- **Crowd** (`MT.babble.crowd(kind, intensity)`):\n  - Cheer: 6–10 detuned \"woo\"/\"yay\" vowel voices plus noise.\n  - Ooh: a falling \"oo\" chorus.\n  - Laugh: \"ha\" syllables at 5 Hz in a chorus of 4.\n  - Applause: band-passed noise claps at a random 8–14 a second for 1.5 s.\n  - Boo: low \"oo\".\n  - Ambience: a quiet murmur loop of random soft syllables.\n\n  Intensity scales the voice count and volume.\n\n---\n\n## 11. UI and UX (10-foot, controller-first)\n\n### 11.1 Rules for every screen\n\n- **Readable from 10 feet:** body text uses the 5×7 font at scale 1 on the 320×240-minimum buffer, which is about 31 px\n  tall on a 1080p TV. Headings use scale 2–3 or `E.font.title`. Never use `font: 'tiny'` for anything a player must read.\n- **At most 6 focusable items per screen**, and no tables wider than 3 columns. Long content is split into pages, turned\n  with ←/→ (L1/R1, Q/E). There are no web-style dense data screens.\n- **Focus** is obvious: a gold marquee-bulb frame chasing round the focused item, a scale bump, and a blip sound.\n- **Every screen has a prompt bar** at the bottom: \"✕ SELECT ○ BACK\" in the glyphs of the last device (§4.4). Prompts\n  update the moment the device changes.\n- **Mouse:** hovering moves the focus; a click activates (`input.clicked()` hit-tested against item rectangles); the\n  wheel scrolls lists and turns sliders.\n- **Keyboard:** arrows/WASD move the focus, Enter or Space selects, Esc or Backspace goes back.\n- Keep clear of the engine's corner buttons (`game.cornerArea()`) and the safe areas. Text is never placed over busy art\n  without a panel (`E.ui.box`, alpha 0.85).\n\n### 11.2 The UI kit (`src/30-ui-kit.js`)\n\nCanvas widgets drawn in `r.overlay`, with focus handling written once:\n- `FocusList(items, { x, y, w, rowH })` and `Button`.\n- `Slider(key)`, `Toggle(key)`, `Tabs`, `Pager`.\n- `Banner` (with bulb frame and slide-in), `LowerThird`, `Card` (the episode and NEXT cards), `Popup` (floating text).\n\nInput comes from **any** player's input in the front end (`game.players`), plus mouse hover and clicks. All widgets work\nheadless: they keep their state and draw nothing.\n\n### 11.3 Screens\n\n| Screen | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| **Title** | Studio backdrop with sweeping spotlights; the logo drops in; \"LIVE FROM STUDIO 9\"; a pulsing \"PRESS ✕ / ENTER\"; `E.versionLabel()` and the game version small at the bottom left. After 10 s idle → attract (§12). Touch-only devices get the notice instead (§11.7). |\n| **Main menu** (on the title) | START THE SHOW · EPISODES · HOW TO PLAY · TOP EARNERS · OPTIONS · ABOUT & CREDITS |\n| **Difficulty** | ROOKIE / CONTESTANT / CHAMPION, each with a one-line description and its HP and lives |\n| **Contestant Lobby** (\"TONIGHT'S CONTESTANTS\") | 4 podiums under spotlights. P1 is in. An empty podium says \"PRESS [Options] TO JOIN\" (`game.allowJoin({ max: 4, keys: false })`). Each joined player: ←/→ change look (live portrait), ✕ ready, ○ leave (P1's ○ goes back to the menu). When all joined players are ready: \"LET'S PLAY!\" and a 2 s countdown. |\n| **How to Play** (3 pages) | 1) Controls with live glyphs for the current device. 2) The show: rooms, doors, cash, prizes, ratings, keys. 3) Power-ups: icon, name and one line each, 6 per page (so 2 sub-pages). |\n| **Episodes** | Episode 1 is always unlocked; 2 and 3 unlock when reached (`E.store`). Picking one starts a REHEARSAL: same rules, no high-score entry. |\n| **Top Earners** | `cab.drawTable(r, 'show', y, 'TOP EARNERS')` styled with the logo, on a gold velvet backdrop |\n| **Options** | `game.menu('settings')`: the engine's standard settings screen, with our settings added (§11.5) |\n| **About & Credits** | Paged, big text: what MASH TV is, then the credits; then pages for **Source Information**, **Testing Feedback**, **Engine Feedback** and **Prompt Feedback** (TASK.md), from `src/about.json` (§16) |\n| **Episode card** | A TV title card: \"EPISODE 2\", \"SWEEPS WEEK\", and the set's name, with static wipes, the theme sting and a Lance line |\n| **NEXT ON MASH TV** (map card) | The episode's 3×3 map as broadcast graphics: room nodes with icons, the visited path lit, the chosen route animating, key icons on key rooms, the current node pulsing. 2.5 s; confirm skips. Pausing in play can also show it (pause item \"Episode map\"). |\n| **Prize Recap** | Penny on a stage with a turntable. Per player, a column with a portrait; prizes appear one by one (icon, name, value, a DING, Penny's joke) and the totals count up; then \"EPISODE MVP: {NAME}\" for the most cash. Confirm speeds it up; holding skips. |\n| **Continue** | `cab.continue(10, done)` over a dimmed arena, styled: \"CONTINUE? 9\", \"−50 % WINNINGS\", with any player's prompt |\n| **Game Over** | Sad trombone, the contestants lying `down` under one spotlight, Lance's line, then the qualifying initials |\n| **Initials** | `cab.enter(score, 'show', done, input)` for each qualifying player in turn, in their colour, with their prompt |\n| **Endings A and B, Credits** | Scripted scenes (§8.2), then a credits roll: MASH TV, made with the my-3D2dge engine, original music and sound in code, the model credit, \"Inspired by Smash TV (1990)\", AI Hub |\n| **Session card / end card** | §14.2 |\n\n### 11.4 The in-play HUD (drawn in `r.overlay`, anchored to `r.W` and `r.H`)\n\n- **Top left:** a blinking red \"● LIVE\" bug and the \"MASH 9\" channel bug, small.\n- **Player panels** in the screen corners: P1 top left (under the bugs), P2 top right, P3 bottom left, P4 bottom right.\n  Each is about 96×26 on a dark panel in the player's colour:\n  - portrait (`rig.drawPortrait`, 16 px) and name;\n  - cash, big (\"$123,450\");\n  - HP pips; lives as mini heads;\n  - the weapon icon and its time bar; add-on icons; Showstopper flashbulbs.\n  - When out: \"OUT — PRESS [Start]\".\n- **Top centre:** the RATINGS gauge (a needle on a semicircle, \"×3\", flashing \"PRIME TIME\"), the keys \"★ 4/9\", and the\n  room name (\"1-3 CLAP TRAP\"). During bosses, the boss bar sits under it.\n- **Bottom centre:** the lower third when someone speaks; otherwise the wave dots (●●●○○) and, after a clear, the\n  \"PICK A DOOR!\" prompt.\n- **Banners** (centre, big, bulb-framed): ROOM CLEAR!, PRIME TIME!, YOU WON: …, GOLDEN KEY 3/9, ENCORE!, BANKRUPT!,\n  WE'RE LOSING VIEWERS!, MASHED!\n- **In the world:** exit signs over open doors; floating \"+$500\" pop-ups; \"STYLE +12\"; a P-number arrow over each\n  contestant for the first 3 s of a room and after a respawn.\n- **Mouse reticle:** drawn at the pointer while the mouse aims (a gold crosshair with the player's colour dot). Hide the\n  OS cursor during play with `canvas.style.cursor = 'none'` while the reticle shows; restore it in menus.\n- **Tutorial prompts** (§7.1): a small panel above the lower third, glyph + text, 4 s each, once per run.\n\n### 11.5 Pause menu and settings\n\n- The **engine's standard pause menu** shows on every play scene (`pausable: true`). Add\n  `menu.items: [['Dev mode', openDev], ['Episode map', openMap], ['How to play', openHowTo]]`.\n  - Each item first closes the Shell, which resumes, then opens our overlay. The overlay freezes the simulation itself\n    (§13.1).\n  - Set `menu.quit = 'title'` (the engine asks first).\n- **10-foot CSS:** inject one `<style>` overriding the engine Shell's sizes (it ships 11–14 px text):\n  ```css\n  .esh{font-size:clamp(18px,2.6vh,34px)!important}\n  .esh h2{font-size:clamp(28px,5vh,60px)!important}\n  .esh button{font-size:clamp(17px,2.4vh,30px)!important;padding:.55em .9em!important;min-height:2.3em!important}\n  .esh-k,.esh-m,.esh-row small,.esh output{font-size:clamp(14px,2vh,24px)!important}\n  .esh-mod{width:min(980px,94vw)!important}\n  .esh input[type=range]{width:min(360px,32vw)!important;height:2em!important}\n  ```\n  Theme it with `new E.Game({ … })` and E.Shell's `theme`: ink `#fff7e8`, paper `rgba(20,8,34,.94)`, accent `#ffcc33`,\n  danger `#ff4d6d`, line `rgba(255,204,51,.35)`, titleFont `Impact, \"Arial Black\", system-ui`.\n- **Our settings** (`game.settings.define`, group `'Game'`, saved by the engine):\n\n  | key | Label | Type | Default |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | `aimAssist` | Aim assist (controller) | toggle | on |\n  | `autoFire` | Auto-fire with the right stick | toggle | on |\n  | `camView` | Camera | select Studio (threequarter) / Overhead (topdown) | Studio |\n  | `subtitles` | Subtitles | toggle | on |\n  | `chatter` | Host chatter | select Normal / Less / Off | Normal |\n  | `confetti` | Confetti amount | select Party / Tasteful | Party |\n\n  Button symbols (PlayStation/Xbox) is the engine's `glyphs` setting (patched, §4.4).\n\n### 11.6 Keyboard and mouse feel native\n\nWASD plus mouse aim and a held left click is the PC twin-stick standard. Arrows are the classic keyboard-only shooting.\nSpace slides, E or right click bombs, Q showboats, Esc pauses. Hover moves the focus everywhere; clicks pick menu items,\nlobby looks and map nodes (map nodes are display only). Prompts show keycaps and mouse icons whenever the keyboard or\nmouse was used last.\n\n### 11.7 Touch-only devices\n\n- **Detection** (re-checked when input arrives):\n  `touchOnly = navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0 && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches && no gamepad connected && no key pressed yet`.\n- **No touch controls:** set `input.touchEnabled = false` and `touch: []` on every scene.\n- **The title shows** a framed notice, \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\", with a big **SETTINGS** button\n  (`game.menu('settings')` on tap) and the engine's corner menu button.\n- **The attract mode still starts after 10 s**, and a tap returns to the notice.\n- **Lifted at once** if a pad connects (`gamepadconnected`) or a key is pressed: the title becomes the normal title.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Attract mode\n\n- **Trigger:** 10 s without input on any front-end screen (title, main menu, how-to, episodes, top earners, about, an\n  empty lobby).\n  - \"Input\" means any key, mouse button, mouse movement over 8 px, wheel, touch, gamepad button, or stick past 0.5, on\n    **any** device.\n  - Detect it with an `MT.anyInput()` helper: DOM listeners plus `navigator.getGamepads()` polling, and headless\n    `input.tap` for tests.\n  - Our own idle clock drives it, not `E.Arcade.attract()`, which only reads P1's input.\n- **Exit:** any input stops the demo at once. Restore the music and go back to the title (`game.go('title')`).\n- **What plays:** `scene 'attract'` runs the **real game** with an `AttractDirector`. It plays a list of reels, each a\n  room or boss loaded with a seed, **2 bot contestants** (co-op on show) with cheats, a duration, and a caption in the\n  lower third:\n\n  | # | Reel | Setup | Caption |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | 1 | Cold Open snippet | jumbotron logo, the Announcer line, Lance waves | \"TONIGHT ON MASH TV!\" |\n  | 2 | 1-1 OPENING NUMBER, 18 s | blaster → spread pickup | \"MASH 'EM!\" |\n  | 3 | 1-2 MIME FIELD, 14 s | rockets; the key is forced at 10 s | \"CASH 'EM!\" |\n  | 4 | MASHY, 16 s | starts in phase 2 with its HP cut | \"BOSSES THE SIZE OF A STUDIO!\" |\n  | 5 | 2-2 CONVEYOR CHAOS, 16 s | popcorn + laser; Prime Time forced (audience throws capsules) | \"PRIME TIME = 5× CASH!\" |\n  | 6 | THE BIG SPIN, 16 s | forced segments JACKPOT then TOMATO TIME | \"SPIN THE WHEEL OF MISFORTUNE!\" |\n  | 7 | 3-6 MASCOT MAYHEM, 16 s | gold records + shield; one bot showboats and one fires a Showstopper | \"PLAY TO THE CAMERAS!\" |\n  | 8 | LANCE LUSTRE LIVE!, 16 s | MIC DROP, then a forced cut to The Network's screens | \"NOBODY CANCELS LANCE LUSTRE!\" |\n  | 9 | Prize Recap snippet, 8 s | fake prize list | \"BIG PRIZES!\" |\n  | 10 | TOP EARNERS, 6 s | the table | — |\n\n  It loops (about 2.5 minutes).\n- **Overlay:** the MASH TV bug, \"DEMO PLAY\", and a pulsing \"PRESS ANY BUTTON\" in the glyphs of the last device. Music\n  plays at a lower volume.\n- **Bot cheats:** god mode, infinite Showstoppers, damage ×3, and the reel's weapon. No high scores and no saves.\n- **The bot** (`src/40-bot.js`, also used by tests and the dev \"bot partner\"):\n  - A potential field: repelled by enemies (stronger when close and in front), foe bullets (predicted 0.3 s ahead) and\n    hazards; attracted to pickups (weighted by value), the room centre, and the chosen exit when the room is clear.\n  - It aims at the nearest enemy with target lead and always fires.\n  - Slides when a bullet will hit within 0.25 s; Showstoppers when 25 or more enemies are within 120; showboats when 3\n    or more enemies are within 60 and it's at full HP.\n  - Route choice is set per use (reel, test route, or \"first exit\").\n\n---\n\n## 13. Dev mode (pause menu → DEV MODE; standalone only, never in hub sessions or the attract mode)\n\n### 13.1 Panel\n\n- A canvas overlay over the right 45 % of the screen, 10-foot sized, on the UI kit. The game keeps drawing behind it.\n- **FREEZE** (on by default) stops the simulation while the panel is open. Off, the game runs behind the panel, so\n  slider changes show live.\n- **Navigation:** L1/R1 or Q/E switch tabs; ↑/↓ select; ←/→ adjust (hold to repeat, `input.repeat`); ✕ toggles or\n  activates; △/R resets the selected value; ○/Esc closes.\n- **Mouse:** hover selects, clicking a slider's bar sets the value, the wheel adjusts, and clicking a tab switches.\n- While open, the play scene sets `pausable = false`, so Esc closes the panel instead of pausing, and the preset hook\n  switches inputs to `MASH_MENU`.\n- **Persistence:** values are kept in `E.store('mashtv:tuning')`. **RESET ALL TO DEFAULTS** (with a confirm) and a reset\n  per row.\n- **An orange \"DEV\" badge** shows in the HUD whenever a tuning differs from its default or a cheat is on, so a run is\n  never mistaken for the real balance. High-score entry is disabled while it shows.\n\n### 13.2 Tabs\n\n**TUNING (live sliders, `MT.T`)**\n\n| key | Label | Default | Range / step |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `playerSpeed` | Player speed | 95 | 50–160 / 5 |\n| `fireRate` | Blaster shots per second | 9 | 3–20 / 1 |\n| `bulletSpeed` | Player bullet speed | 330 | 150–600 / 10 |\n| `dmgMul` | Player damage × | 1 | 0.25–4 / 0.25 |\n| `slideSpeed` | Slide speed | 235 | 120–400 / 5 |\n| `slideCooldown` | Slide cooldown (s) | 0.85 | 0.2–3 / 0.05 |\n| `invulnTime` | Invulnerability after a hit (s) | 1.2 | 0.2–3 / 0.1 |\n| `hpPerLife` | HP per life (0 = the difficulty's) | 0 | 0–9 / 1 |\n| `startLives` | Starting lives (0 = the difficulty's) | 0 | 0–9 / 1 |\n| `puDuration` | Power-up duration × | 1 | 0.25–3 / 0.25 |\n| `enemySpeed` | Enemy speed × | 1 | 0.25–2.5 / 0.05 |\n| `enemyHp` | Enemy HP × | 1 | 0.25–4 / 0.25 |\n| `enemyBulletSpeed` | Enemy bullet speed × | 1 | 0.25–2.5 / 0.05 |\n| `contactDmg` | Contact damage | 1 | 0–3 / 1 |\n| `waveSize` | Wave size × | 1 | 0.25–3 / 0.05 |\n| `spawnGap` | Spawn gap × | 1 | 0.25–3 / 0.05 |\n| `bossHp` | Boss HP × | 1 | 0.1–4 / 0.1 |\n| `dropMul` | Drop chance × | 1 | 0–5 / 0.25 |\n| `cashMul` | Cash × | 1 | 0.25–5 / 0.25 |\n| `ratingsGain` | Ratings gain × | 1 | 0–5 / 0.25 |\n| `ratingsDecay` | Ratings decay per second | 2.5 | 0–10 / 0.5 |\n| `primeTime` | Prime Time length (s) | 12 | 3–30 / 1 |\n| `camZoom` | Camera zoom × | 1 | 0.6–1.6 / 0.05 |\n| `shakeMul` | Screen shake × | 1 | 0–2 / 0.1 |\n| `timeScale` | Game speed (`game.timeScale`) | 1 | 0.1–3 / 0.05 |\n\n- Page the 25 sliders 6 at a time, in groups: PLAYER, ENEMIES, ECONOMY, SHOW, CAMERA & TIME.\n- **CHEATS** (toggles): god mode (all contestants), infinite Showstoppers, one-hit kills, freeze enemies, no clip,\n  autopilot P1 (the bot drives P1), bot partner (adds a bot contestant), mute host chatter.\n- **ACTIONS:**\n  - Kill all enemies; clear the room (jump to EXIT); next room (first exit); restart the room.\n  - Give a weapon (choose from the list), give an add-on; +1 life; +1 Showstopper; +$100,000; +1 key.\n  - Max ratings; trigger Prime Time; trigger a Ratings Stunt.\n  - Frame step: one press advances 1/30 s while frozen.\n- **WARP:** every room (1-1 … 3-B), the Green Room, the Lightning Round rooms, and the scenes Cold Open, Episode card\n  1/2/3, Prize Recap 1/2/3, Continue, Game Over, Initials, Ending A, Ending B, Credits, and a session card preview (fake\n  players, no hub).\n- **SPAWN:** an enemy kind × count (1/5/10) × where (N/E/S/W/drop/audience/at the reticle); a pickup kind at P1.\n- **DEBUG** (overlays): hitboxes (bodies, bullets, parts, triggers), AI targets and states, the wave timeline (current\n  wave, next trigger, alive count), stats (fps, update steps per frame, entities, bullets, particles), door states, and\n  the camera bounds.\nPLAN_EOF\nwc -l PLAN.md","description":"Append presentation, audio, UI, attract and dev sections"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":320,"cache_read_input_tokens":414244,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":320},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"messages_changed","cache_missed_input_tokens":311966}},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17","uuid":"ad93dacd-a9db-475e-9a8f-646e1b389d96","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:38:34.287Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh2ebU4zUfqcC6hJwLzA","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01TbQZHLyHmTWuApC1YvwMwp":{"command":"cat >> PLAN.md <<'PLAN_EOF'\n\n---\n\n## 9. Presentation: art direction, assets, animation, effects\n\n### 9.1 Art direction\n\n- **The engine's native look is the base:** `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true`; HD Humanoid rigs at size 1.0–1.6, every\n  one dressed; textured TileMap floors and walls; `r.shape` props; canvas lighting on every scene (`enableGPU({ map })`\n  as well where WebGPU exists, with the same ambients); particles and `game.hitFx` on every hit; `E.font.title` logos;\n  `E.tones` shading on everything drawn by hand.\n- **On top of it, a retro-future TV studio:** neon, chrome, marquee bulbs, sponsor logos, confetti, spotlights, a\n  jumbotron. Check every screenshot against \"a Saturday-morning game show crossed with a PS1 arcade port\", never\n  against an NES game.\n- **Palette** (shade every colour with `E.tones`):\n\n  | Role | Colours |\n  |---|---|\n  | Shadow | `#140a24`, `#2a1446` |\n  | Neon | magenta `#ff2e88`, cyan `#2ee6ff` |\n  | Gold | `#ffcc33` |\n  | Chrome | `#c8d0e0` |\n  | Tally light | red `#ff3344` |\n  | Players | P1 `#e8443a`, P2 `#3b82f6`, P3 `#22c55e`, P4 `#f5b700` (the hub's seat colours, also used standalone) |\n  | Foe bullets | warm orange `#ff9a3c` and yellow cores, with dark outlines, never in player colours |\n\n- Information never rides on colour alone: players have numbers on their backs and HUD panels, and pickups carry letters.\n\n### 9.2 Sets (one per episode, plus the Green Room)\n\nEach set provides a floor texture function, a wall decorator, a door painter, an ambient and lights, and an **outside\ndressing** list. The outside dressing fills the space a wide or tall screen shows beyond the arena walls, in world\ncoordinates outside 0–368 × 0–240:\n- **Bleachers:** E and W, x −130 to −10 and 378 to 498. Stepped boxes with rows of audience sprites.\n- **North:** a lighting truss and the jumbotron at (184, −36, z 46), 120 × 64 units.\n- **South:** a camera dolly track with two camera cranes at (54, 272) and (314, 272), plus front-row audience.\n\n| Set | Floor | Walls and doors | Props in the arena (keep play space clear) | Outside | Light |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `studio` (Ep1, Lightning Round) | glossy black-purple tiles with magenta and gold inlay; the MASH TV logo painted in the centre (§9.4) | flats in purple velvet with gold trim and neon strips; marquee bulbs chasing along the top edge; shutter doors in gold frames | 4 spotlight stands in the corners; hazard stripes at doors | bleachers, audience, jumbotron, camera cranes, APPLAUSE and ON AIR signs | ambient 0.55; 4 coloured spotlights sweeping slowly; door beacons |\n| `warehouse` (Ep2) | sealed concrete with yellow safety lines and pallet scuffs | corrugated metal with MegaMart banners; roll-up loading-dock doors with red and green beacons | stacks of prize crates and gift boxes in the corners (asset store `kenney-holiday` presents, crates); conveyor belts in 2-2 | shelving racks of prizes (fridges, TVs and sofas from the store), a forklift, audience behind a chain-link barrier | ambient 0.6; cold fluorescent strips; amber beacons |\n| `rooftop` (Ep3) | dark slate tiles, a red carpet down the middle, a gold \"M\" helipad ring | glass balustrades with gold posts and velvet ropes; doors are elevator doors with brass frames | champagne towers, potted palms, a searchlight base in two corners | the city skyline glowing at the far edge (teal and amber window lights); searchlights; fireworks bursting in the sky every few seconds | ambient 0.4 (night); warm string lights; fireworks flashes; the searchlight cones |\n| `greenroom` | emerald carpet with a gold pattern | wood panelling with framed headshots of Lance | sofas, a fridge, a cake table, TVs (asset store) | — | ambient 0.6; warm lamps |\n\n### 9.3 Characters\n\n| Character | Build | Look |\n|---|---|---|\n| ROCKET (P1 default) | Humanoid `heroic`, size 1.1, `weapon: 'gun'` | red jumpsuit (outfit `shirt`, pants the same colour), white trim stripes, short hair, black boots and gloves, a white \"1\" on the back |\n| VOLT (P2) | as above | blue jumpsuit, `ponytail`, a goggles band (`hat: 'band'`), silver gloves, \"2\" |\n| MOXIE (P3) | as above | green jumpsuit, `spiky` hair, `cap`, \"3\" |\n| GOLDIE (P4) | as above | yellow jumpsuit, `long` hair, headband, \"4\" |\n| LANCE LUSTRE | `heroic`, size 1.3 | `spiky` silver pompadour, purple sequinned `coat` (gold trim; sparkle glints in draw), black pants, white gloves, a gold microphone drawn at `rig.hand()`. Portrait: an exaggerated grin with a tooth glint. |\n| PENNY PRIZE | `heroic`, size 1.2 | `long` pink hair, teal sequinned gown (`outfit: 'robe'`), white gloves, glitter particles |\n| Audience | small string sprites, 8 head-and-shoulders variants × `E.recolor` palettes | they bob, cheer (arms up), do the wave, and some hold signs (\"MASH!\", \"KA-CHING!\", \"HI MOM\") |\n| Paparazzi | Humanoid `chibi`, size 0.9 | trench coat, cap, a camera drawn at the hands (a flash on firing) |\n\n- **Contestants pick a look in the lobby;** the slot fixes the colour.\n- **In hub sessions** the look follows the seat, and the hub's names replace the flavour names.\n- **Enemy and boss looks** are in §5.8 and §5.9. Their build:\n  - **Humanoid** (outfits and palettes per kind): goon `bulky` 1.0; popper `chibi` 1.0 with a party hat; mime `heroic`\n    1.0 with white face paint, a striped shirt and a beret; heckler `chibi` 0.95 with a jersey and foam finger; brute\n    `bulky` 1.6 with a giant bear-mascot head and a cereal-box chest emblem.\n  - **Blob:** clapbot is a metallic `Blob` with drawn clapper hands and an antenna.\n  - **Custom painters** (`r.queue` + `E.px` polys shaded with `E.tones`): the disco ball (faceted sphere, rotating\n    glints), the dolly tank (box chassis, scrolling treads, camera-lens turret), the cable snake (striped cable discs and\n    a plug head) and every boss.\n\n### 9.4 New art assets to author (list them in `src/06-art-studio.js`, `07-art-chars.js` and `08-sprites.js`)\n\n**Floors** (floorTex functions):\n1. Studio gloss tiles.\n2. The **logo decal.** Rasterize \"MASH TV\" with `E.font.title` into an offscreen canvas once, then sample that canvas\n   inside the floorTex for the centre 160×60 area.\n3. Warehouse concrete with lines.\n4. Rooftop slate with the red carpet strip and helipad ring.\n5. Green Room carpet.\n\n**Walls and doors:**\n6. Wall decorators (`types[id].decorate(face)`): velvet flats with gold trim, corrugated metal with a banner, glass\n   balustrade, wood panelling.\n7. A door painter for 4 sides × 5 states. Shutter rise animation, beacons, and the exit sign with the destination text\n   and icon.\n8. Marquee-bulb chase strips (top of the walls, menu frames, the jumbotron border), drawn per frame as dots.\n\n**Studio dressing and props** (shape entries per `docs/ASSETS.md`, checked with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs`):\n9. Camera crane, studio camera on a tripod, spotlight stand, truss segment, jumbotron frame, APPLAUSE sign, ON AIR\n   sign, prize pedestal (turning top), prize cage, bleacher block, sponsor billboard, champagne tower, velvet rope\n   stanchion, searchlight, a vault safe with locks (3-4), and a forklift.\n10. Store props cut into one set with `tools/asset-set.mjs`: presents, crates, barrels, fridge, televisions, sofas,\n    cakes, speakers, lamps.\n\n**Characters and bosses:**\n11. Contestant, host and co-host rig definitions.\n12. Enemy painters and attachments: clapper hands, the popper's party popper, the mime's face paint, the brute's mascot\n    head, the tank, the disco ball, the snake.\n13. Boss painters: MASHY (body, arms, treads, hatch, eyes), THE WHEEL (8 labelled segments, bulbs, pointer, mount),\n    STARMAKER 9000 (podium, hover jets, turrets), THE NETWORK (a 4×3 TV wall and THE EYE).\n\n**Sprites** (string sprites, 8–16 px):\n14. Bullets for each weapon and foe kind; the rocket and popcorn bag; kernels; the gold record.\n15. Cash ×4, prize boxes ×3, key, capsule (a mini TV with icon slots), flashbulb, 1UP star, statuette, and the 18 prize\n    icons (12×12).\n16. HUD icons: weapons, hearts and HP pips, lives (mini contestant heads), keys, the room-type icons (sword, $, gun,\n    flame, star, skull), the LIVE dot, the MASH 9 bug.\n17. **Glyph icons** (§4.4): PlayStation, Xbox, keycaps, mouse.\n\n**Logos and decals:**\n18. The MASH TV logo: `E.font.title` \"MASH TV\" in a magenta→gold gradient with depth, inside a drawn CRT-TV outline\n    with bunny-ear antennas and a sparkle. Plus the \"LIVE FROM STUDIO 9\" sub-line.\n19. Decals: tomato splats, scorch marks, confetti on the floor that slowly fades.\n\n### 9.5 Animation (the engine's system as the base, plus new motion where the source needs it)\n\n**Contestants:** gait from velocity (automatic), and `point: true` with the facing on the aim when shooting.\n- The slide is a dive roll (`flip` 0→1).\n- Showboat cycles `cheer`, `hips` and `wave` with `expr: 'smile'`.\n- Hurt flinch (`hurt`), then death (`'die'`, then `down: 1`).\n- The respawn drop: a cable line from the top of the screen, a body descending, a land squash (`land: k`).\n- Victory `cheer`.\n- The walk-in at room start.\n\n**Host and co-host:** talking gestures, cycling `wave`, `hips` and `point` (the mic raised). On the jumbotron\nportraits, the mouth moves while text types (the Dialog portrait technique: `rig.drawPortrait`, with `expr` set while\ntalking).\n\n**Enemies:**\n- Goon: baton `E.MOVES.bash` with a 0.3 s wind; a stagger on hit.\n- Clapbot: hop squash and stretch (`blob.kick`), with hands clapping on a sine.\n- Popper: waddle, then a fuse swell (a scale tween and a red flash).\n- Mime: **\"trapped in a box\"**, a new animation. After `rig.update`, post-process the joints: hands flat at chest and\n  head height, sliding along invisible walls on a slow sine (wrap `rig.solvePose()` as `AI_GUIDE.md` describes). The\n  telegraph is a frantic shake.\n- Heckler: a leap arc, then the throw (`E.MOVES.throw`).\n- Brute: the stomp telegraph (`crouch`, then `kick` squash, rings), a charge lean (`sprint`), and a stunned wobble with\n  orbiting stars.\n- Tank: tread scroll speed from its velocity, turret slew, recoil on firing.\n- Snake: segments follow the head's path, with a sine weave.\n- Disco: facet glints rotate with its spin.\n\n**Bosses:** Mashy's arms aim and recoil, the treads scroll, the hatch tweens open, the googly eyes jiggle. The Wheel's\nspin uses angle easing (`outCubic`) down to the stop, with pointer clicks on each segment boundary. The Starmaker bobs,\nits jets flicker, and the mic drop is a rise-and-slam tween. The Network's screens flip channels with a static wipe, and\nTHE EYE dilates.\n\n**Studio:** audience bob amplitude grows with the ratings (the wave runs in Prime Time). Spotlights sweep. Marquee bulbs\nchase. The jumbotron has scanlines. Confetti cannons fire.\n\n**UI:** the logo drops in with `outBounce`. Menu focus has a pulsing bulb frame. Banners slide and bounce, and the\nnumbers count up. The \"TV static\" transition is 0.4 s of animated noise via `px.blend` plus a horizontal roll.\n\n**Check** every attack and pose with `node tools/filmstrip.mjs` (`--seed 1`): a wind-up, a strike and a clean settle.\n\n### 9.6 Effects, lighting, feel\n\n- **Every hit:** a hit flash and small sparks. **Every kill:** a confetti `particles.bits` in the enemy palette, a pop,\n  a cash pop-up (`particles.text`).\n  - Big kills (tank, brute, boss parts): `particles.explosion` + `game.shake(4)` + a `game.flash` for bosses.\n- **Hit-stop:** `game.freeze(.03)` on brute, tank and boss-part kills and on contestant hits. **Never in hub sessions**:\n  it would freeze the host's world for everyone.\n- **Lights:** each set's ambient, plus `game.lights.add` for spotlights, door beacons (red or green), bullet glows\n  (emissive), pyro flames, the laser and the jumbotron's glow. The Prime Time strobe follows the comfort setting.\n- **Comfort:** every shake, flash and strobe goes through `game.shake` and `game.flash`, which respect reduce motion,\n  screen shake and screen flashes.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Audio (everything synthesized in code)\n\n### 10.1 Music (engine notation songs in `src/03-audio-music.js`, all original)\n\n| id | Used for | Tempo and feel | Layers (`audio.layer`) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `theme` | title, Cold Open, episode cards | 132 bpm, a brassy game-show fanfare in C major, swing hi-hats | — |\n| `studioBattle` | Ep1 rooms, Lightning Round | 150 bpm driving funk: slap bass on triangle, square lead, drums | `prime` (brass stabs, `on: false`), `intense` (extra drums when 30+ enemies are alive) |\n| `warehouseFunk` | Ep2 rooms | 128 bpm disco funk, four-on-the-floor | `prime`, `intense` |\n| `finaleOverture` | Ep3 rooms | 160 bpm dramatic minor, saw strings | `prime`, `intense` |\n| `bossShowdown` | all bosses | 170 bpm, minor, relentless | `phase2` (brought in by later phases) |\n| `commercial` | Commercial Break, sponsor ads, Prize Recap intro | 112 bpm cheesy jingle | — |\n| `recap` | Prize Recap, Green Room | 100 bpm lounge (sine Rhodes, brushed drums) | — |\n| `ending` | endings and credits | 96 bpm, a warm reprise of the theme | — |\n\n- **Stings** (`audio.sting`): room clear \"ta-da\", prize fanfare, key chime, Prime Time fanfare, extra life, BANKRUPT\n  (descending trombone), Game Over (sad trombone), continue tick, \"MASH!\" start hit, boss-defeat fanfare.\n- A starting motif for the theme (develop it into 16+ bars; the whole score is original):\n  - lead: `G4 . C5 . E5 . G5 - - . E5 . G5 . A5 - | G5 - - - E5 - C5 - D5 - - - . . . .`\n  - bass: `C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 . A2 . A2 . F2 . G2 .`\n  - drums: `k . h . s . h . k k h . s . h o`\n- Use `music(song, { fade: .6 })` for crossfades. `audio.duck` comes from the engine during pause; duck the music\n  yourself under host lines too (−40 %).\n\n### 10.2 Sound effects (`audio.define` in `src/04-audio-sfx.js`)\n\n| Group | Sounds |\n|---|---|\n| Weapons | blaster pew, spread triple, rocket launch and boom, popcorn thunk and kernel pops, laser hum (re-triggered every 0.12 s while firing), records whirr, shield hum, sneakers squeak |\n| Contestants | slide whoosh, showboat flourish, Showstopper flashbulb pop + boom, hurt buzzer (\"wrong answer\"), death (descending \"wah\" + crowd \"ooh\"), respawn cable zip and land, cash ka-ching, prize fanfare, key chime, power-up rising arp, power-up expire click, extra life |\n| Enemies | goon grunt and baton whoosh, clapbot hop and clap, popper fuse hiss and pop, mime beep and pfft, disco shimmer and beam zap, heckler \"boo\" and tomato splat, tank lens whine and shell boom, snake hiss and segment snap, brute roar, stomp and wall crash, confetti pop on death |\n| Studio | door klaxon, door roll-up and slam, beacon tick, conveyor hum and reverse alarm, pyro whoosh, crowd ambience loop, applause, cheer, laugh, ooh, boo, drum roll, wheel click, TV static, channel flip, camera shutter |\n| UI | focus move (a soft blip), confirm (a bell), back, page flip, slider tick, typing blips (when no voice), countdown beeps, continue tick |\n\nUse the engine's voice objects (`wave`, `freq`, `to`, `dur`, `arp`, `vib`, filtered `noise`). Give each sound a\ndistinct contour, so you can tell weapons apart blind.\n\n### 10.3 Babble: Animalese/Simlish voices (`src/05-babble.js`)\n\nA small formant-synthesis voice engine on `game.audio.ctx`, routed through the engine's effects bus.\n- **Engine patch P4:** add a getter `game.audio.bus` that returns `_sfxBus` after `_init()`, so the voice follows the\n  volume and mute settings.\n\n**`MT.babble.say(text, voiceId, { speed, emotion })`:**\n- Schedules syllables from `ctx.currentTime` and returns the line's duration. Headless (no context), it returns the\n  duration and does nothing.\n- **Syllables:** split the text into words, and each word into syllables at vowel groups (cap 4 per word). Each\n  syllable's sounds come from a seeded hash of the word, so the same line always sounds the same.\n- **Each syllable:**\n  - An onset consonant by class: plosive (a 15 ms band-passed noise burst at 2–4 kHz), fricative (40–70 ms high-passed\n    noise), nasal (a 40 ms low sine) or none.\n  - A vowel nucleus, 60–120 ms at the voice's speed. The source is a sawtooth or pulse at `f0`, through 2 parallel\n    band-pass filters at the vowel's formants (a 800/1200, e 400/2300, i 300/2800, o 450/800, u 325/700; Q 8),\n    shifted by the voice's `formant` factor.\n  - A gain envelope: 5 ms attack, 30 ms release.\n- **Pitch:** `f0 = base × contour`.\n  - Words rise toward a `?`, fall at the end of a sentence, and jump +20 % for `!` or ALL-CAPS emphasis.\n  - Jitter ±4 % per syllable, plus a vibrato of 5.5 Hz at 2 %.\n  - Pauses: 80 ms at a comma, 160 ms at `.`, `!` or `?`, 300 ms at `...`.\n- **Voices:**\n\n  | id | base f0 | speed (syll/s) | wave | formant × | character |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|---|\n  | `lance` | 135 Hz | 13 | saw | 1.0 | fast, swooping, smarmy (big rises on \"!\") |\n  | `penny` | 300 Hz | 12 | pulse | 1.18 | bubbly, with giggle trills (arp at line ends) |\n  | `annc` | 85 Hz | 7 | saw | 0.85 | slow and booming, with a feedback-delay echo |\n  | `network` | 70 Hz | 8 | square | 0.8 | monotone, a waveshaper bitcrush, robotic |\n  | `mashy` | 110 Hz | 9 | triangle | 0.9 | goofy and bouncy |\n  | `contestant1–4` | 160–240 Hz | 12 | pulse | 1.05 | short exclamations: \"HUP!\", \"YES!\" |\n  | `goon` | 95 Hz | 10 | saw | 0.9 | grunts on spawn and hit, at most 2 at once |\n\n- **Subtitles:** the lower third types at a speed tied to the babble duration, so text and voice end together.\n- **Crowd** (`MT.babble.crowd(kind, intensity)`):\n  - Cheer: 6–10 detuned \"woo\"/\"yay\" vowel voices plus noise.\n  - Ooh: a falling \"oo\" chorus.\n  - Laugh: \"ha\" syllables at 5 Hz in a chorus of 4.\n  - Applause: band-passed noise claps at a random 8–14 a second for 1.5 s.\n  - Boo: low \"oo\".\n  - Ambience: a quiet murmur loop of random soft syllables.\n\n  Intensity scales the voice count and volume.\n\n---\n\n## 11. UI and UX (10-foot, controller-first)\n\n### 11.1 Rules for every screen\n\n- **Readable from 10 feet:** body text uses the 5×7 font at scale 1 on the 320×240-minimum buffer, which is about 31 px\n  tall on a 1080p TV. Headings use scale 2–3 or `E.font.title`. Never use `font: 'tiny'` for anything a player must read.\n- **At most 6 focusable items per screen**, and no tables wider than 3 columns. Long content is split into pages, turned\n  with ←/→ (L1/R1, Q/E). There are no web-style dense data screens.\n- **Focus** is obvious: a gold marquee-bulb frame chasing round the focused item, a scale bump, and a blip sound.\n- **Every screen has a prompt bar** at the bottom: \"✕ SELECT ○ BACK\" in the glyphs of the last device (§4.4). Prompts\n  update the moment the device changes.\n- **Mouse:** hovering moves the focus; a click activates (`input.clicked()` hit-tested against item rectangles); the\n  wheel scrolls lists and turns sliders.\n- **Keyboard:** arrows/WASD move the focus, Enter or Space selects, Esc or Backspace goes back.\n- Keep clear of the engine's corner buttons (`game.cornerArea()`) and the safe areas. Text is never placed over busy art\n  without a panel (`E.ui.box`, alpha 0.85).\n\n### 11.2 The UI kit (`src/30-ui-kit.js`)\n\nCanvas widgets drawn in `r.overlay`, with focus handling written once:\n- `FocusList(items, { x, y, w, rowH })` and `Button`.\n- `Slider(key)`, `Toggle(key)`, `Tabs`, `Pager`.\n- `Banner` (with bulb frame and slide-in), `LowerThird`, `Card` (the episode and NEXT cards), `Popup` (floating text).\n\nInput comes from **any** player's input in the front end (`game.players`), plus mouse hover and clicks. All widgets work\nheadless: they keep their state and draw nothing.\n\n### 11.3 Screens\n\n| Screen | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| **Title** | Studio backdrop with sweeping spotlights; the logo drops in; \"LIVE FROM STUDIO 9\"; a pulsing \"PRESS ✕ / ENTER\"; `E.versionLabel()` and the game version small at the bottom left. After 10 s idle → attract (§12). Touch-only devices get the notice instead (§11.7). |\n| **Main menu** (on the title) | START THE SHOW · EPISODES · HOW TO PLAY · TOP EARNERS · OPTIONS · ABOUT & CREDITS |\n| **Difficulty** | ROOKIE / CONTESTANT / CHAMPION, each with a one-line description and its HP and lives |\n| **Contestant Lobby** (\"TONIGHT'S CONTESTANTS\") | 4 podiums under spotlights. P1 is in. An empty podium says \"PRESS [Options] TO JOIN\" (`game.allowJoin({ max: 4, keys: false })`). Each joined player: ←/→ change look (live portrait), ✕ ready, ○ leave (P1's ○ goes back to the menu). When all joined players are ready: \"LET'S PLAY!\" and a 2 s countdown. |\n| **How to Play** (3 pages) | 1) Controls with live glyphs for the current device. 2) The show: rooms, doors, cash, prizes, ratings, keys. 3) Power-ups: icon, name and one line each, 6 per page (so 2 sub-pages). |\n| **Episodes** | Episode 1 is always unlocked; 2 and 3 unlock when reached (`E.store`). Picking one starts a REHEARSAL: same rules, no high-score entry. |\n| **Top Earners** | `cab.drawTable(r, 'show', y, 'TOP EARNERS')` styled with the logo, on a gold velvet backdrop |\n| **Options** | `game.menu('settings')`: the engine's standard settings screen, with our settings added (§11.5) |\n| **About & Credits** | Paged, big text: what MASH TV is, then the credits; then pages for **Source Information**, **Testing Feedback**, **Engine Feedback** and **Prompt Feedback** (TASK.md), from `src/about.json` (§16) |\n| **Episode card** | A TV title card: \"EPISODE 2\", \"SWEEPS WEEK\", and the set's name, with static wipes, the theme sting and a Lance line |\n| **NEXT ON MASH TV** (map card) | The episode's 3×3 map as broadcast graphics: room nodes with icons, the visited path lit, the chosen route animating, key icons on key rooms, the current node pulsing. 2.5 s; confirm skips. Pausing in play can also show it (pause item \"Episode map\"). |\n| **Prize Recap** | Penny on a stage with a turntable. Per player, a column with a portrait; prizes appear one by one (icon, name, value, a DING, Penny's joke) and the totals count up; then \"EPISODE MVP: {NAME}\" for the most cash. Confirm speeds it up; holding skips. |\n| **Continue** | `cab.continue(10, done)` over a dimmed arena, styled: \"CONTINUE? 9\", \"−50 % WINNINGS\", with any player's prompt |\n| **Game Over** | Sad trombone, the contestants lying `down` under one spotlight, Lance's line, then the qualifying initials |\n| **Initials** | `cab.enter(score, 'show', done, input)` for each qualifying player in turn, in their colour, with their prompt |\n| **Endings A and B, Credits** | Scripted scenes (§8.2), then a credits roll: MASH TV, made with the my-3D2dge engine, original music and sound in code, the model credit, \"Inspired by Smash TV (1990)\", AI Hub |\n| **Session card / end card** | §14.2 |\n\n### 11.4 The in-play HUD (drawn in `r.overlay`, anchored to `r.W` and `r.H`)\n\n- **Top left:** a blinking red \"● LIVE\" bug and the \"MASH 9\" channel bug, small.\n- **Player panels** in the screen corners: P1 top left (under the bugs), P2 top right, P3 bottom left, P4 bottom right.\n  Each is about 96×26 on a dark panel in the player's colour:\n  - portrait (`rig.drawPortrait`, 16 px) and name;\n  - cash, big (\"$123,450\");\n  - HP pips; lives as mini heads;\n  - the weapon icon and its time bar; add-on icons; Showstopper flashbulbs.\n  - When out: \"OUT — PRESS [Start]\".\n- **Top centre:** the RATINGS gauge (a needle on a semicircle, \"×3\", flashing \"PRIME TIME\"), the keys \"★ 4/9\", and the\n  room name (\"1-3 CLAP TRAP\"). During bosses, the boss bar sits under it.\n- **Bottom centre:** the lower third when someone speaks; otherwise the wave dots (●●●○○) and, after a clear, the\n  \"PICK A DOOR!\" prompt.\n- **Banners** (centre, big, bulb-framed): ROOM CLEAR!, PRIME TIME!, YOU WON: …, GOLDEN KEY 3/9, ENCORE!, BANKRUPT!,\n  WE'RE LOSING VIEWERS!, MASHED!\n- **In the world:** exit signs over open doors; floating \"+$500\" pop-ups; \"STYLE +12\"; a P-number arrow over each\n  contestant for the first 3 s of a room and after a respawn.\n- **Mouse reticle:** drawn at the pointer while the mouse aims (a gold crosshair with the player's colour dot). Hide the\n  OS cursor during play with `canvas.style.cursor = 'none'` while the reticle shows; restore it in menus.\n- **Tutorial prompts** (§7.1): a small panel above the lower third, glyph + text, 4 s each, once per run.\n\n### 11.5 Pause menu and settings\n\n- The **engine's standard pause menu** shows on every play scene (`pausable: true`). Add\n  `menu.items: [['Dev mode', openDev], ['Episode map', openMap], ['How to play', openHowTo]]`.\n  - Each item first closes the Shell, which resumes, then opens our overlay. The overlay freezes the simulation itself\n    (§13.1).\n  - Set `menu.quit = 'title'` (the engine asks first).\n- **10-foot CSS:** inject one `<style>` overriding the engine Shell's sizes (it ships 11–14 px text):\n  ```css\n  .esh{font-size:clamp(18px,2.6vh,34px)!important}\n  .esh h2{font-size:clamp(28px,5vh,60px)!important}\n  .esh button{font-size:clamp(17px,2.4vh,30px)!important;padding:.55em .9em!important;min-height:2.3em!important}\n  .esh-k,.esh-m,.esh-row small,.esh output{font-size:clamp(14px,2vh,24px)!important}\n  .esh-mod{width:min(980px,94vw)!important}\n  .esh input[type=range]{width:min(360px,32vw)!important;height:2em!important}\n  ```\n  Theme it with `new E.Game({ … })` and E.Shell's `theme`: ink `#fff7e8`, paper `rgba(20,8,34,.94)`, accent `#ffcc33`,\n  danger `#ff4d6d`, line `rgba(255,204,51,.35)`, titleFont `Impact, \"Arial Black\", system-ui`.\n- **Our settings** (`game.settings.define`, group `'Game'`, saved by the engine):\n\n  | key | Label | Type | Default |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | `aimAssist` | Aim assist (controller) | toggle | on |\n  | `autoFire` | Auto-fire with the right stick | toggle | on |\n  | `camView` | Camera | select Studio (threequarter) / Overhead (topdown) | Studio |\n  | `subtitles` | Subtitles | toggle | on |\n  | `chatter` | Host chatter | select Normal / Less / Off | Normal |\n  | `confetti` | Confetti amount | select Party / Tasteful | Party |\n\n  Button symbols (PlayStation/Xbox) is the engine's `glyphs` setting (patched, §4.4).\n\n### 11.6 Keyboard and mouse feel native\n\nWASD plus mouse aim and a held left click is the PC twin-stick standard. Arrows are the classic keyboard-only shooting.\nSpace slides, E or right click bombs, Q showboats, Esc pauses. Hover moves the focus everywhere; clicks pick menu items,\nlobby looks and map nodes (map nodes are display only). Prompts show keycaps and mouse icons whenever the keyboard or\nmouse was used last.\n\n### 11.7 Touch-only devices\n\n- **Detection** (re-checked when input arrives):\n  `touchOnly = navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0 && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches && no gamepad connected && no key pressed yet`.\n- **No touch controls:** set `input.touchEnabled = false` and `touch: []` on every scene.\n- **The title shows** a framed notice, \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED\", with a big **SETTINGS** button\n  (`game.menu('settings')` on tap) and the engine's corner menu button.\n- **The attract mode still starts after 10 s**, and a tap returns to the notice.\n- **Lifted at once** if a pad connects (`gamepadconnected`) or a key is pressed: the title becomes the normal title.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Attract mode\n\n- **Trigger:** 10 s without input on any front-end screen (title, main menu, how-to, episodes, top earners, about, an\n  empty lobby).\n  - \"Input\" means any key, mouse button, mouse movement over 8 px, wheel, touch, gamepad button, or stick past 0.5, on\n    **any** device.\n  - Detect it with an `MT.anyInput()` helper: DOM listeners plus `navigator.getGamepads()` polling, and headless\n    `input.tap` for tests.\n  - Our own idle clock drives it, not `E.Arcade.attract()`, which only reads P1's input.\n- **Exit:** any input stops the demo at once. Restore the music and go back to the title (`game.go('title')`).\n- **What plays:** `scene 'attract'` runs the **real game** with an `AttractDirector`. It plays a list of reels, each a\n  room or boss loaded with a seed, **2 bot contestants** (co-op on show) with cheats, a duration, and a caption in the\n  lower third:\n\n  | # | Reel | Setup | Caption |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | 1 | Cold Open snippet | jumbotron logo, the Announcer line, Lance waves | \"TONIGHT ON MASH TV!\" |\n  | 2 | 1-1 OPENING NUMBER, 18 s | blaster → spread pickup | \"MASH 'EM!\" |\n  | 3 | 1-2 MIME FIELD, 14 s | rockets; the key is forced at 10 s | \"CASH 'EM!\" |\n  | 4 | MASHY, 16 s | starts in phase 2 with its HP cut | \"BOSSES THE SIZE OF A STUDIO!\" |\n  | 5 | 2-2 CONVEYOR CHAOS, 16 s | popcorn + laser; Prime Time forced (audience throws capsules) | \"PRIME TIME = 5× CASH!\" |\n  | 6 | THE BIG SPIN, 16 s | forced segments JACKPOT then TOMATO TIME | \"SPIN THE WHEEL OF MISFORTUNE!\" |\n  | 7 | 3-6 MASCOT MAYHEM, 16 s | gold records + shield; one bot showboats and one fires a Showstopper | \"PLAY TO THE CAMERAS!\" |\n  | 8 | LANCE LUSTRE LIVE!, 16 s | MIC DROP, then a forced cut to The Network's screens | \"NOBODY CANCELS LANCE LUSTRE!\" |\n  | 9 | Prize Recap snippet, 8 s | fake prize list | \"BIG PRIZES!\" |\n  | 10 | TOP EARNERS, 6 s | the table | — |\n\n  It loops (about 2.5 minutes).\n- **Overlay:** the MASH TV bug, \"DEMO PLAY\", and a pulsing \"PRESS ANY BUTTON\" in the glyphs of the last device. Music\n  plays at a lower volume.\n- **Bot cheats:** god mode, infinite Showstoppers, damage ×3, and the reel's weapon. No high scores and no saves.\n- **The bot** (`src/40-bot.js`, also used by tests and the dev \"bot partner\"):\n  - A potential field: repelled by enemies (stronger when close and in front), foe bullets (predicted 0.3 s ahead) and\n    hazards; attracted to pickups (weighted by value), the room centre, and the chosen exit when the room is clear.\n  - It aims at the nearest enemy with target lead and always fires.\n  - Slides when a bullet will hit within 0.25 s; Showstoppers when 25 or more enemies are within 120; showboats when 3\n    or more enemies are within 60 and it's at full HP.\n  - Route choice is set per use (reel, test route, or \"first exit\").\n\n---\n\n## 13. Dev mode (pause menu → DEV MODE; standalone only, never in hub sessions or the attract mode)\n\n### 13.1 Panel\n\n- A canvas overlay over the right 45 % of the screen, 10-foot sized, on the UI kit. The game keeps drawing behind it.\n- **FREEZE** (on by default) stops the simulation while the panel is open. Off, the game runs behind the panel, so\n  slider changes show live.\n- **Navigation:** L1/R1 or Q/E switch tabs; ↑/↓ select; ←/→ adjust (hold to repeat, `input.repeat`); ✕ toggles or\n  activates; △/R resets the selected value; ○/Esc closes.\n- **Mouse:** hover selects, clicking a slider's bar sets the value, the wheel adjusts, and clicking a tab switches.\n- While open, the play scene sets `pausable = false`, so Esc closes the panel instead of pausing, and the preset hook\n  switches inputs to `MASH_MENU`.\n- **Persistence:** values are kept in `E.store('mashtv:tuning')`. **RESET ALL TO DEFAULTS** (with a confirm) and a reset\n  per row.\n- **An orange \"DEV\" badge** shows in the HUD whenever a tuning differs from its default or a cheat is on, so a run is\n  never mistaken for the real balance. High-score entry is disabled while it shows.\n\n### 13.2 Tabs\n\n**TUNING (live sliders, `MT.T`)**\n\n| key | Label | Default | Range / step |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `playerSpeed` | Player speed | 95 | 50–160 / 5 |\n| `fireRate` | Blaster shots per second | 9 | 3–20 / 1 |\n| `bulletSpeed` | Player bullet speed | 330 | 150–600 / 10 |\n| `dmgMul` | Player damage × | 1 | 0.25–4 / 0.25 |\n| `slideSpeed` | Slide speed | 235 | 120–400 / 5 |\n| `slideCooldown` | Slide cooldown (s) | 0.85 | 0.2–3 / 0.05 |\n| `invulnTime` | Invulnerability after a hit (s) | 1.2 | 0.2–3 / 0.1 |\n| `hpPerLife` | HP per life (0 = the difficulty's) | 0 | 0–9 / 1 |\n| `startLives` | Starting lives (0 = the difficulty's) | 0 | 0–9 / 1 |\n| `puDuration` | Power-up duration × | 1 | 0.25–3 / 0.25 |\n| `enemySpeed` | Enemy speed × | 1 | 0.25–2.5 / 0.05 |\n| `enemyHp` | Enemy HP × | 1 | 0.25–4 / 0.25 |\n| `enemyBulletSpeed` | Enemy bullet speed × | 1 | 0.25–2.5 / 0.05 |\n| `contactDmg` | Contact damage | 1 | 0–3 / 1 |\n| `waveSize` | Wave size × | 1 | 0.25–3 / 0.05 |\n| `spawnGap` | Spawn gap × | 1 | 0.25–3 / 0.05 |\n| `bossHp` | Boss HP × | 1 | 0.1–4 / 0.1 |\n| `dropMul` | Drop chance × | 1 | 0–5 / 0.25 |\n| `cashMul` | Cash × | 1 | 0.25–5 / 0.25 |\n| `ratingsGain` | Ratings gain × | 1 | 0–5 / 0.25 |\n| `ratingsDecay` | Ratings decay per second | 2.5 | 0–10 / 0.5 |\n| `primeTime` | Prime Time length (s) | 12 | 3–30 / 1 |\n| `camZoom` | Camera zoom × | 1 | 0.6–1.6 / 0.05 |\n| `shakeMul` | Screen shake × | 1 | 0–2 / 0.1 |\n| `timeScale` | Game speed (`game.timeScale`) | 1 | 0.1–3 / 0.05 |\n\n- Page the 25 sliders 6 at a time, in groups: PLAYER, ENEMIES, ECONOMY, SHOW, CAMERA & TIME.\n- **CHEATS** (toggles): god mode (all contestants), infinite Showstoppers, one-hit kills, freeze enemies, no clip,\n  autopilot P1 (the bot drives P1), bot partner (adds a bot contestant), mute host chatter.\n- **ACTIONS:**\n  - Kill all enemies; clear the room (jump to EXIT); next room (first exit); restart the room.\n  - Give a weapon (choose from the list), give an add-on; +1 life; +1 Showstopper; +$100,000; +1 key.\n  - Max ratings; trigger Prime Time; trigger a Ratings Stunt.\n  - Frame step: one press advances 1/30 s while frozen.\n- **WARP:** every room (1-1 … 3-B), the Green Room, the Lightning Round rooms, and the scenes Cold Open, Episode card\n  1/2/3, Prize Recap 1/2/3, Continue, Game Over, Initials, Ending A, Ending B, Credits, and a session card preview (fake\n  players, no hub).\n- **SPAWN:** an enemy kind × count (1/5/10) × where (N/E/S/W/drop/audience/at the reticle); a pickup kind at P1.\n- **DEBUG** (overlays): hitboxes (bodies, bullets, parts, triggers), AI targets and states, the wave timeline (current\n  wave, next trigger, alive count), stats (fps, update steps per frame, entities, bullets, particles), door states, and\n  the camera bounds.\nPLAN_EOF\nwc -l PLAN.md","description":"Append presentation, audio, UI, attract and dev sections"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TbQZHLyHmTWuApC1YvwMwp","type":"tool_result","content":"1483 PLAN.md\n[This command modified 1 file you've previously read: PLAN.md. Call Read before editing.]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17","uuid":"a7eb1c6a-7489-45b0-b7ff-aec57fd9b20f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:38:39.348Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1483 PLAN.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"staleReadFileStateHint":"[This command modified 1 file you've previously read: PLAN.md. Call Read before editing.]","bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183312__xf5fb84/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":260,"oldLines":3,"newStart":260,"newLines":4,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":50,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":50,\"session_id\":\"a4347fe7-6622-4d1f-b2e4-5b62026e02e2\",\"uuid\":\"7520e896-de65-4b4a-8f87-28460306c030\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":350,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":300,\"session_id\":\"a4347fe7-6622-4d1f-b2e4-5b62026e02e2\",\"uuid\":\"9b8b2d00-dd8b-49fd-90c7-4e110a7d6bbb\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":650,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":300,\"session_id\":\"a4347fe7-6622-4d1f-b2e4-5b62026e02e2\",\"uuid\":\"b8e48d0a-e3c2-4b18-844f-b158076ec1d8\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":900,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":250,\"session_id\":\"a4347fe7-6622-4d1f-b2e4-5b62026e02e2\",\"uuid\":\"f8931303-40c6-4c8d-861a-ce7e82ada0ee\"}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174728__x978410/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":2022,"oldLines":3,"newStart":2022,"newLines":5,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":266,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":116,\"session_id\":\"0af7cd7c-7b61-489d-86ab-010715e3fe6f\",\"uuid\":\"cb9143a1-0004-4aa1-9a8e-fceec97b43d0\"}"," {\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cgh2qt9iicoDuer15Lf27\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"\",\"signature\":\"\"}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1872,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":465290,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":1872},\"output_tokens\":7,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"0af7cd7c-7b61-489d-86ab-010715e3fe6f\",\"uuid\":\"c80ca81c-9534-4bec-a1f6-2bde57aebe61\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:37:53.409Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh2qsrcNVGvGE6wRf6RP\",\"thinking_duration_ms\":2325,\"thinking_display\":\"updates\"}"," {\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cgh2qt9iicoDuer15Lf27\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Last part: the appendices (reference spline code, the voice algorithm, the planning-script results, a hub session skeleton, World3D facts).\"}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1872,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":465290,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":1872},\"output_tokens\":7,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"0af7cd7c-7b61-489d-86ab-010715e3fe6f\",\"uuid\":\"76067fab-17ac-4a87-8421-40a51d21da84\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:37:53.410Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh2qsrcNVGvGE6wRf6RP\"}","+{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cgh2qt9iicoDuer15Lf27\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01JPy5zpvne7PYpkH3QnNUaP\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"cat >> /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174728__x978410/work/PLAN.md <<'EOF'\\n## Appendix A: reference code (use as is)\\n\\n**Centripetal Catmull-Rom (closed) and the arc-length resample.** This is the code the planning checker used. The §4.3\\nnumbers come from it.\\n\\n```js\\n// P: [[x, y, z], ...] closed loop; returns dense samples [[x, y, z, segIndex], ...] (k per segment)\\nfunction catmullClosed(P, k = 64) {\\n  const n = P.length, out = [], d = (a, b) => Math.hypot(b[0] - a[0], b[1] - a[1], b[2] - a[2]);\\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\n    const p0 = P[(i - 1 + n) % n], p1 = P[i], p2 = P[(i + 1) % n], p3 = P[(i + 2) % n];\\n    const t0 = 0, t1 = Math.sqrt(d(p0, p1)), t2 = t1 + Math.sqrt(d(p1, p2)), t3 = t2 + Math.sqrt(d(p2, p3));   // alpha 0.5\\n    for (let j = 0; j < k; j++) {\\n      const t = t1 + (t2 - t1) * j / k, q = [];\\n      for (let c = 0; c < 3; c++) {\\n        const A1 = (t1 - t) / (t1 - t0) * p0[c] + (t - t0) / (t1 - t0) * p1[c];\\n        const A2 = (t2 - t) / (t2 - t1) * p1[c] + (t - t1) / (t2 - t1) * p2[c];\\n        const A3 = (t3 - t) / (t3 - t2) * p2[c] + (t - t2) / (t3 - t2) * p3[c];\\n        const B1 = (t2 - t) / (t2 - t0) * A1 + (t - t0) / (t2 - t0) * A2;\\n        const B2 = (t3 - t) / (t3 - t1) * A2 + (t - t1) / (t3 - t1) * A3;\\n        q.push((t2 - t) / (t2 - t1) * B1 + (t - t1) / (t2 - t1) * B2);\\n      }\\n      out.push([q[0], q[1], q[2], i]);                 // (j === 0: exactly control point i: its knot)\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return out;\\n}\\n// resample at a fixed spacing ds; returns { L, ds, pts, knots } (knots[i] = s of control point i)\\nfunction resampleClosed(dense, ds) {\\n  const n = dense.length, acc = [0], knots = [];\\n  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) { const a = dense[i - 1], b = dense[i % n]; acc.push(acc[i - 1] + Math.hypot(b[0] - a[0], b[1] - a[1], b[2] - a[2])); }\\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (i === 0 || dense[i][3] !== dense[i - 1][3]) knots[dense[i][3]] = acc[i];\\n  const L = acc[n], m = Math.round(L / ds), step = L / m, pts = [];\\n  for (let k = 0, j = 0; k < m; k++) {\\n    const s = k * step; while (acc[j + 1] < s) j++;\\n    const a = dense[j], b = dense[(j + 1) % n], f = (s - acc[j]) / (acc[j + 1] - acc[j]);\\n    pts.push([a[0] + (b[0] - a[0]) * f, a[1] + (b[1] - a[1]) * f, a[2] + (b[2] - a[2]) * f]);\\n  }\\n  return { L, ds: step, pts, knots };\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**Frame helper (engine axes, left-handed: right = cross(up, forward)):**\\n\\n```js\\nconst cross = (a, b) => [a[1] * b[2] - a[2] * b[1], a[2] * b[0] - a[0] * b[2], a[0] * b[1] - a[1] * b[0]];\\nconst norm = a => { const l = Math.hypot(a[0], a[1], a[2]) || 1; return [a[0] / l, a[1] / l, a[2] / l]; };\\nSN.frame = (F, up = [0, 0, 1], bank = 0) => {          // -> { F, R, U }; bank > 0 lowers the right edge\\n  const R0 = norm(cross(up, F)), U0 = cross(F, R0), c = Math.cos(bank), s = Math.sin(bank);\\n  return { F, R: [R0[0] * c - U0[0] * s, R0[1] * c - U0[1] * s, R0[2] * c - U0[2] * s],\\n              U: [U0[0] * c + R0[0] * s, U0[1] * c + R0[1] * s, U0[2] * c + R0[2] * s] };\\n};\\n// test: SN.frame([1,0,0]).R ≈ [0,1,0] (south = right when heading east); SN.frame([1,0,0],[0,0,1],.3).R[2] < 0 (right edge lower)\\n```\\n\\n**World3D camera pose from a frame:**\\n- `yaw = atan2(dir.y, dir.x)` and `pitch = asin(dir.z)` (negative looks down), where `dir` = unit(look − eye);\\n- `roll = rollSign · atan2(dot(Ucam, Rflat), dot(Ucam, Zup))`, with `Rflat` = unit(cross(Zup, dir)) under the engine\\n  convention.\\n\\nSet `rollSign` by the M3 screenshot check.\\n\\n## Appendix B: gibberish voice (`SN.Voice`)\\n\\n```js\\n// say(text, v = { f0, speed, ring, crush, vol }) -> seconds; headless: computes the length, makes no sound\\nfunction say(text, v) {\\n  const words = text.toUpperCase().replace(/[^A-Z0-9?!.' ]/g, ' ').split(/\\\\s+/).filter(Boolean);\\n  let t = 0; const ctx = game.audio.ctx, out = sfxBus();            // the engine's effects bus: volume settings apply\\n  for (const w of words) {\\n    const rnd = E.rng(hashString(w)), n = Math.max(1, Math.round(w.replace(/[^A-Z]/g, '').length / 2.6));\\n    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\n      const vowel = 'AEIOU'['AEIOUY'.indexOf(w[(i * 2 + 1) % w.length]) >= 0 ? 'AEIOU'.indexOf(w[(i * 2 + 1) % w.length]) : (rnd() * 5) | 0];\\n      const dur = (0.07 + rnd() * 0.04) / v.speed, last = i === n - 1;\\n      const bend = last && /[.]$/.test(w) ? 0.88 : last && /[?]$/.test(w) ? 1.18 : last && /!$/.test(w) ? 1.10 : 1;\\n      const f = v.f0 * (0.92 + rnd() * 0.16) * bend;\\n      if (ctx) { consonant(ctx, out, t, 2000 + rnd() * 3000, 0.018); vowelTone(ctx, out, t + 0.012, dur, f, FORMANTS[vowel], v); }\\n      t += dur + 0.012;\\n    }\\n    t += 0.06 / v.speed;                                            // a gap between words\\n  }\\n  return t;\\n}\\n// FORMANTS = { A: [800, 1200], E: [400, 2000], I: [300, 2300], O: [450, 800], U: [325, 700] }\\n// vowelTone: saw osc at f (+ vibrato 6 Hz, 1.5 %) -> two bandpass filters (F1, F2; Q 6) summed -> gain env (8 ms attack,\\n// 30 ms release) [-> ring mod (osc * 60 Hz) for ORACLE; -> WaveShaper step curve for KAI-ZERO]; plus 15 % noise.\\n```\\n\\n## Appendix C: planning-script results (`tools/trackcheck.mjs` must reproduce them)\\n\\nSpline: App. A, k = 48–96, resampled at 2 m (0.5 m for the jumps). Air gravity 40 m/s². Flights start from the lip\\nwith the lip's slope, 0.9 m above the surface.\\n\\n| Track | Length | Tightest radius | Slopes (outside jumps) | Overpass | Jump |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Port Solenne | 3.84 km | 56 m (s ≈ 3412, lighthouse) | −5 … +5 % | none | drop cp12→14: an 8.6 m step, 0.65 s air at all speeds; impact 45 m/s −30°, 90 m/s −16°, 170 m/s −6° |\\n| Karakum Spire | 4.59 km | 55 m (s ≈ 2408, the spiral) | −8 … +7 % | s 2208 under s 2928: 24.5 m | gap cp7→9, 57 m: 45 m/s hits the wall 20.6 m low, 60 m/s 6.6 m low, **75 m/s clears** (0 m past, −9°), 90 m/s +18 m (−13°), 115 +49 m, 140 +82 m, 170 +129 m (−10°) |\\n| Meridian Stack | 4.18 km | 52 m (s ≈ 4167, before the start straight) | −4 … +4 % | s 2082 under s 3983: 31.9 m | drop cp4→6: a 14.2 m step; 45 m/s lands at −37° (hard), 90 m/s −21°, 140 m/s −12°, 170 m/s −11° |\\n| Borealis Vault | 5.03 km | 44 m (s ≈ 3774, switchbacks) | −6 … +11 % | none | drop cp6→8: a 17.2 m step; 45 m/s −40° (hard), 90 m/s −23°, 115 m/s −15°, 170 m/s −12° (1.2 s air) |\\n\\nLap-time estimates are in §4.2. Hard landings (> 25°) cost energy and speed by design (§3.2 step 5). Slow craft\\nare punished, fast ones reward.\\n\\n## Appendix D: AI Hub session skeleton (`90-hub.js`)\\n\\n```js\\nSN.hub = { active: !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active), info: null, ghosts: new Map(), lastSend: 0, ended: false, reported: false };\\nasync function startSession() {                               // called from the boot's last step when SN.hub.active\\n  const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true });\\n  if (!info) { SN.hub.active = false; return game.go('title'); }\\n  SN.hub.info = info;\\n  const ev = SN.hub.event = { track: ['solenne', 'karakum', 'meridian', 'borealis'][Math.floor(AIHub.rng('event')() * 4)],\\n                              cls: 'FLARE', laps: 3, seed: Math.floor(AIHub.rng('race')() * 2 ** 32) };\\n  for (const p of info.players) if (p.id !== info.me) SN.hub.ghosts.set(p.id, { id: p.id, name: p.name, color: p.color, s: [] });\\n  AIHub.on('card', c => SN.hub.card = c);\\n  AIHub.on('start', () => SN.hub.started = true);\\n  AIHub.on('message', (d, from) => { const g = SN.hub.ghosts.get(from); if (g && d.t === 'g') g.s.push(Object.assign({ at: AIHub.time() }, d)); if (g && d.t === 'done') g.done = d.ms; });\\n  AIHub.on('leave', ({ id }) => SN.hub.ghosts.delete(id));\\n  AIHub.on('time-up', () => { if (!SN.hub.reported) { SN.hub.reported = true; AIHub.result({ score: Math.round(myShip().p / 16) }); } });\\n  AIHub.on('end', e => { SN.hub.ended = true; SN.hub.endInfo = e; SN.audio.stopAll(); });\\n  game.go('loading', { next: 'session', track: ev.track, then: () => AIHub.ready() });   // ready() once the world is warm\\n}\\n// in the session scene's update: card UI until started; countdown from AIHub.time(); race.step only when time() >= 0 and !ended;\\n// every 100 ms: AIHub.send({ t: 'g', p, u, h, y, r, v, lap, fin }, { reliable: false }); every 1000 ms: AIHub.progress({ score, label });\\n// on finish: AIHub.result({ time: Math.round(AIHub.time()), stats, awards }); AIHub.send({ t: 'done', ms }); SN.hub.reported = true\\n```\\n\\n## Appendix E: World3D facts learned in planning (saves the builders an afternoon)\\n\\n- The 3D add-on is an ES module that imports `three/webgpu`, `three/tsl` and `@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat` by bare\\n  specifier. It needs an import map. It attaches `E.World3D`, `E.THREE`, `E.TSL` and `E.RAPIER`, and throws if the 2D\\n  engine isn't loaded first.\\n- `await E.World3D.init(game)` creates one renderer for the page: WebGPU, else WebGL 2 (`?backend=webgl` forces it).\\n  It makes `#view` under `#screen` if missing. The game must use `bg: null`. It always initialises Rapier (so Rapier\\n  must be loadable even though craft don't use it). Headless it returns at once.\\n- `new E.World3D(game, { terrain | level, sky, fog, ambient, sun, lights, glowLights, shadows, filter, camera,\\n  onDraw, overlay })`.\\n  - `terrain` coordinates run from 0 to `size` (a power-of-two chunk count): keep tracks inside (hence `origin`).\\n  - `world.scene` takes your own three.js meshes. Use **node materials only**: MeshLambert / MeshToon / MeshBasic\\n    NodeMaterial. Never ShaderMaterial, WebGLRenderer or addons.\\n  - Make the materials at world creation. `world.prepare()` builds the shaders. `world.dispose()` before making the\\n    next world.\\n- The camera has modes `view`, `chase`, `first`, `fly`, `fixed`, `zones` and `behind`. We use `fixed` with\\n  `camera.fixed = { x, y, z, yaw, pitch, roll, fov }`. `camera.far` and `camera.near` are in units.\\n  `world.addCamera(o)` plus `game.splitScreen(2)` makes a split screen (filters are off while split).\\n  `world.project(x, y, z)` gives HUD pixels.\\n- `world.step(dt)` in update and `world.draw(r)` in draw, then the HUD with `r.overlay`.\\n- `game.particles.*` draw in 3D and land on `particles.ground`. World3D resets that every draw: see edit §2.7.5.\\n- Its settings keys: `look3d`, `picture3d` (`pixels` | `balanced` | `full`), `shadows3d`, `filter3d` (`clean` |\\n  `cel` | `pixel`). `filter.bloom` is a boolean in the world's `filter`.\\n- Its own racing (`world.track`, `world.vehicle`, a Rapier ball) is **not** used. The craft need track-space physics\\n  at Wipeout speeds (§3.2), and the tracks need tunnels, drops, gaps and overpasses that `world.track` doesn't model.\\nEOF\\nwc -l /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174728__x978410/work/PLAN.md\",\"description\":\"Append appendices A–E to PLAN.md\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1872,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":465290,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":1872},\"output_tokens\":7,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"0af7cd7c-7b61-489d-86ab-010715e3fe6f\",\"uuid\":\"cd27be36-4788-414e-8a57-6789820d8f80\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:38:38.326Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh2qsrcNVGvGE6wRf6RP\",\"wire_tool_inputs\":{\"toolu_01JPy5zpvne7PYpkH3QnNUaP\":{\"command\":\"cat >> /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174728__x978410/work/PLAN.md <<'EOF'\\n## Appendix A: reference code (use as is)\\n\\n**Centripetal Catmull-Rom (closed) and the arc-length resample.** This is the code the planning checker used. The §4.3\\nnumbers come from it.\\n\\n```js\\n// P: [[x, y, z], ...] closed loop; returns dense samples [[x, y, z, segIndex], ...] (k per segment)\\nfunction catmullClosed(P, k = 64) {\\n  const n = P.length, out = [], d = (a, b) => Math.hypot(b[0] - a[0], b[1] - a[1], b[2] - a[2]);\\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\n    const p0 = P[(i - 1 + n) % n], p1 = P[i], p2 = P[(i + 1) % n], p3 = P[(i + 2) % n];\\n    const t0 = 0, t1 = Math.sqrt(d(p0, p1)), t2 = t1 + Math.sqrt(d(p1, p2)), t3 = t2 + Math.sqrt(d(p2, p3));   // alpha 0.5\\n    for (let j = 0; j < k; j++) {\\n      const t = t1 + (t2 - t1) * j / k, q = [];\\n      for (let c = 0; c < 3; c++) {\\n        const A1 = (t1 - t) / (t1 - t0) * p0[c] + (t - t0) / (t1 - t0) * p1[c];\\n        const A2 = (t2 - t) / (t2 - t1) * p1[c] + (t - t1) / (t2 - t1) * p2[c];\\n        const A3 = (t3 - t) / (t3 - t2) * p2[c] + (t - t2) / (t3 - t2) * p3[c];\\n        const B1 = (t2 - t) / (t2 - t0) * A1 + (t - t0) / (t2 - t0) * A2;\\n        const B2 = (t3 - t) / (t3 - t1) * A2 + (t - t1) / (t3 - t1) * A3;\\n        q.push((t2 - t) / (t2 - t1) * B1 + (t - t1) / (t2 - t1) * B2);\\n      }\\n      out.push([q[0], q[1], q[2], i]);                 // (j === 0: exactly control point i: its knot)\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return out;\\n}\\n// resample at a fixed spacing ds; returns { L, ds, pts, knots } (knots[i] = s of control point i)\\nfunction resampleClosed(dense, ds) {\\n  const n = dense.length, acc = [0], knots = [];\\n  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) { const a = dense[i - 1], b = dense[i % n]; acc.push(acc[i - 1] + Math.hypot(b[0] - a[0], b[1] - a[1], b[2] - a[2])); }\\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (i === 0 || dense[i][3] !== dense[i - 1][3]) knots[dense[i][3]] = acc[i];\\n  const L = acc[n], m = Math.round(L / ds), step = L / m, pts = [];\\n  for (let k = 0, j = 0; k < m; k++) {\\n    const s = k * step; while (acc[j + 1] < s) j++;\\n    const a = dense[j], b = dense[(j + 1) % n], f = (s - acc[j]) / (acc[j + 1] - acc[j]);\\n    pts.push([a[0] + (b[0] - a[0]) * f, a[1] + (b[1] - a[1]) * f, a[2] + (b[2] - a[2]) * f]);\\n  }\\n  return { L, ds: step, pts, knots };\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**Frame helper (engine axes, left-handed: right = cross(up, forward)):**\\n\\n```js\\nconst cross = (a, b) => [a[1] * b[2] - a[2] * b[1], a[2] * b[0] - a[0] * b[2], a[0] * b[1] - a[1] * b[0]];\\nconst norm = a => { const l = Math.hypot(a[0], a[1], a[2]) || 1; return [a[0] / l, a[1] / l, a[2] / l]; };\\nSN.frame = (F, up = [0, 0, 1], bank = 0) => {          // -> { F, R, U }; bank > 0 lowers the right edge\\n  const R0 = norm(cross(up, F)), U0 = cross(F, R0), c = Math.cos(bank), s = Math.sin(bank);\\n  return { F, R: [R0[0] * c - U0[0] * s, R0[1] * c - U0[1] * s, R0[2] * c - U0[2] * s],\\n              U: [U0[0] * c + R0[0] * s, U0[1] * c + R0[1] * s, U0[2] * c + R0[2] * s] };\\n};\\n// test: SN.frame([1,0,0]).R ≈ [0,1,0] (south = right when heading east); SN.frame([1,0,0],[0,0,1],.3).R[2] < 0 (right edge lower)\\n```\\n\\n**World3D camera pose from a frame:**\\n- `yaw = atan2(dir.y, dir.x)` and `pitch = asin(dir.z)` (negative looks down), where `dir` = unit(look − eye);\\n- `roll = rollSign · atan2(dot(Ucam, Rflat), dot(Ucam, Zup))`, with `Rflat` = unit(cross(Zup, dir)) under the engine\\n  convention.\\n\\nSet `rollSign` by the M3 screenshot check.\\n\\n## Appendix B: gibberish voice (`SN.Voice`)\\n\\n```js\\n// say(text, v = { f0, speed, ring, crush, vol }) -> seconds; headless: computes the length, makes no sound\\nfunction say(text, v) {\\n  const words = text.toUpperCase().replace(/[^A-Z0-9?!.' ]/g, ' ').split(/\\\\s+/).filter(Boolean);\\n  let t = 0; const ctx = game.audio.ctx, out = sfxBus();            // the engine's effects bus: volume settings apply\\n  for (const w of words) {\\n    const rnd = E.rng(hashString(w)), n = Math.max(1, Math.round(w.replace(/[^A-Z]/g, '').length / 2.6));\\n    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\\n      const vowel = 'AEIOU'['AEIOUY'.indexOf(w[(i * 2 + 1) % w.length]) >= 0 ? 'AEIOU'.indexOf(w[(i * 2 + 1) % w.length]) : (rnd() * 5) | 0];\\n      const dur = (0.07 + rnd() * 0.04) / v.speed, last = i === n - 1;\\n      const bend = last && /[.]$/.test(w) ? 0.88 : last && /[?]$/.test(w) ? 1.18 : last && /!$/.test(w) ? 1.10 : 1;\\n      const f = v.f0 * (0.92 + rnd() * 0.16) * bend;\\n      if (ctx) { consonant(ctx, out, t, 2000 + rnd() * 3000, 0.018); vowelTone(ctx, out, t + 0.012, dur, f, FORMANTS[vowel], v); }\\n      t += dur + 0.012;\\n    }\\n    t += 0.06 / v.speed;                                            // a gap between words\\n  }\\n  return t;\\n}\\n// FORMANTS = { A: [800, 1200], E: [400, 2000], I: [300, 2300], O: [450, 800], U: [325, 700] }\\n// vowelTone: saw osc at f (+ vibrato 6 Hz, 1.5 %) -> two bandpass filters (F1, F2; Q 6) summed -> gain env (8 ms attack,\\n// 30 ms release) [-> ring mod (osc * 60 Hz) for ORACLE; -> WaveShaper step curve for KAI-ZERO]; plus 15 % noise.\\n```\\n\\n## Appendix C: planning-script results (`tools/trackcheck.mjs` must reproduce them)\\n\\nSpline: App. A, k = 48–96, resampled at 2 m (0.5 m for the jumps). Air gravity 40 m/s². Flights start from the lip\\nwith the lip's slope, 0.9 m above the surface.\\n\\n| Track | Length | Tightest radius | Slopes (outside jumps) | Overpass | Jump |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Port Solenne | 3.84 km | 56 m (s ≈ 3412, lighthouse) | −5 … +5 % | none | drop cp12→14: an 8.6 m step, 0.65 s air at all speeds; impact 45 m/s −30°, 90 m/s −16°, 170 m/s −6° |\\n| Karakum Spire | 4.59 km | 55 m (s ≈ 2408, the spiral) | −8 … +7 % | s 2208 under s 2928: 24.5 m | gap cp7→9, 57 m: 45 m/s hits the wall 20.6 m low, 60 m/s 6.6 m low, **75 m/s clears** (0 m past, −9°), 90 m/s +18 m (−13°), 115 +49 m, 140 +82 m, 170 +129 m (−10°) |\\n| Meridian Stack | 4.18 km | 52 m (s ≈ 4167, before the start straight) | −4 … +4 % | s 2082 under s 3983: 31.9 m | drop cp4→6: a 14.2 m step; 45 m/s lands at −37° (hard), 90 m/s −21°, 140 m/s −12°, 170 m/s −11° |\\n| Borealis Vault | 5.03 km | 44 m (s ≈ 3774, switchbacks) | −6 … +11 % | none | drop cp6→8: a 17.2 m step; 45 m/s −40° (hard), 90 m/s −23°, 115 m/s −15°, 170 m/s −12° (1.2 s air) |\\n\\nLap-time estimates are in §4.2. Hard landings (> 25°) cost energy and speed by design (§3.2 step 5). Slow craft\\nare punished, fast ones reward.\\n\\n## Appendix D: AI Hub session skeleton (`90-hub.js`)\\n\\n```js\\nSN.hub = { active: !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active), info: null, ghosts: new Map(), lastSend: 0, ended: false, reported: false };\\nasync function startSession() {                               // called from the boot's last step when SN.hub.active\\n  const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true });\\n  if (!info) { SN.hub.active = false; return game.go('title'); }\\n  SN.hub.info = info;\\n  const ev = SN.hub.event = { track: ['solenne', 'karakum', 'meridian', 'borealis'][Math.floor(AIHub.rng('event')() * 4)],\\n                              cls: 'FLARE', laps: 3, seed: Math.floor(AIHub.rng('race')() * 2 ** 32) };\\n  for (const p of info.players) if (p.id !== info.me) SN.hub.ghosts.set(p.id, { id: p.id, name: p.name, color: p.color, s: [] });\\n  AIHub.on('card', c => SN.hub.card = c);\\n  AIHub.on('start', () => SN.hub.started = true);\\n  AIHub.on('message', (d, from) => { const g = SN.hub.ghosts.get(from); if (g && d.t === 'g') g.s.push(Object.assign({ at: AIHub.time() }, d)); if (g && d.t === 'done') g.done = d.ms; });\\n  AIHub.on('leave', ({ id }) => SN.hub.ghosts.delete(id));\\n  AIHub.on('time-up', () => { if (!SN.hub.reported) { SN.hub.reported = true; AIHub.result({ score: Math.round(myShip().p / 16) }); } });\\n  AIHub.on('end', e => { SN.hub.ended = true; SN.hub.endInfo = e; SN.audio.stopAll(); });\\n  game.go('loading', { next: 'session', track: ev.track, then: () => AIHub.ready() });   // ready() once the world is warm\\n}\\n// in the session scene's update: card UI until started; countdown from AIHub.time(); race.step only when time() >= 0 and !ended;\\n// every 100 ms: AIHub.send({ t: 'g', p, u, h, y, r, v, lap, fin }, { reliable: false }); every 1000 ms: AIHub.progress({ score, label });\\n// on finish: AIHub.result({ time: Math.round(AIHub.time()), stats, awards }); AIHub.send({ t: 'done', ms }); SN.hub.reported = true\\n```\\n\\n## Appendix E: World3D facts learned in planning (saves the builders an afternoon)\\n\\n- The 3D add-on is an ES module that imports `three/webgpu`, `three/tsl` and `@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat` by bare\\n  specifier. It needs an import map. It attaches `E.World3D`, `E.THREE`, `E.TSL` and `E.RAPIER`, and throws if the 2D\\n  engine isn't loaded first.\\n- `await E.World3D.init(game)` creates one renderer for the page: WebGPU, else WebGL 2 (`?backend=webgl` forces it).\\n  It makes `#view` under `#screen` if missing. The game must use `bg: null`. It always initialises Rapier (so Rapier\\n  must be loadable even though craft don't use it). Headless it returns at once.\\n- `new E.World3D(game, { terrain | level, sky, fog, ambient, sun, lights, glowLights, shadows, filter, camera,\\n  onDraw, overlay })`.\\n  - `terrain` coordinates run from 0 to `size` (a power-of-two chunk count): keep tracks inside (hence `origin`).\\n  - `world.scene` takes your own three.js meshes. Use **node materials only**: MeshLambert / MeshToon / MeshBasic\\n    NodeMaterial. Never ShaderMaterial, WebGLRenderer or addons.\\n  - Make the materials at world creation. `world.prepare()` builds the shaders. `world.dispose()` before making the\\n    next world.\\n- The camera has modes `view`, `chase`, `first`, `fly`, `fixed`, `zones` and `behind`. We use `fixed` with\\n  `camera.fixed = { x, y, z, yaw, pitch, roll, fov }`. `camera.far` and `camera.near` are in units.\\n  `world.addCamera(o)` plus `game.splitScreen(2)` makes a split screen (filters are off while split).\\n  `world.project(x, y, z)` gives HUD pixels.\\n- `world.step(dt)` in update and `world.draw(r)` in draw, then the HUD with `r.overlay`.\\n- `game.particles.*` draw in 3D and land on `particles.ground`. World3D resets that every draw: see edit §2.7.5.\\n- Its settings keys: `look3d`, `picture3d` (`pixels` | `balanced` | `full`), `shadows3d`, `filter3d` (`clean` |\\n  `cel` | `pixel`). `filter.bloom` is a boolean in the world's `filter`.\\n- Its own racing (`world.track`, `world.vehicle`, a Rapier ball) is **not** used. The craft need track-space physics\\n  at Wipeout speeds (§3.2), and the tracks need tunnels, drops, gaps and overpasses that `world.track` doesn't model.\\nEOF\\nwc -l /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174728__x978410/work/PLAN.md\",\"description\":\"Append appendices A–E to PLAN.md\"}}}","+{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01JPy5zpvne7PYpkH3QnNUaP\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"2025 /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174728__x978410/work/PLAN.md\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"0af7cd7c-7b61-489d-86ab-010715e3fe6f\",\"uuid\":\"4642d56f-60b7-48ba-81a6-071b5060f96d\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:38:38.345Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"stdout\":\"2025 /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174728__x978410/work/PLAN.md\",\"stderr\":\"\",\"interrupted\":false,\"isImage\":false,\"noOutputExpected\":false},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_01JPy5zpvne7PYpkH3QnNUaP\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174728__x978410/work/PLAN.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":1871,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1871,"newLines":155,"lines":[" "," ---"," ","+## Appendix A: reference code (use as is)","+","+**Centripetal Catmull-Rom (closed) and the arc-length resample.** This is the code the planning checker used. The §4.3","+numbers come from it.","+","+```js","+// P: [[x, y, z], ...] closed loop; returns dense samples [[x, y, z, segIndex], ...] (k per segment)","+function catmullClosed(P, k = 64) {","+  const n = P.length, out = [], d = (a, b) => Math.hypot(b[0] - a[0], b[1] - a[1], b[2] - a[2]);","+  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {","+    const p0 = P[(i - 1 + n) % n], p1 = P[i], p2 = P[(i + 1) % n], p3 = P[(i + 2) % n];","+    const t0 = 0, t1 = Math.sqrt(d(p0, p1)), t2 = t1 + Math.sqrt(d(p1, p2)), t3 = t2 + Math.sqrt(d(p2, p3));   // alpha 0.5","+    for (let j = 0; j < k; j++) {","+      const t = t1 + (t2 - t1) * j / k, q = [];","+      for (let c = 0; c < 3; c++) {","+        const A1 = (t1 - t) / (t1 - t0) * p0[c] + (t - t0) / (t1 - t0) * p1[c];","+        const A2 = (t2 - t) / (t2 - t1) * p1[c] + (t - t1) / (t2 - t1) * p2[c];","+        const A3 = (t3 - t) / (t3 - t2) * p2[c] + (t - t2) / (t3 - t2) * p3[c];","+        const B1 = (t2 - t) / (t2 - t0) * A1 + (t - t0) / (t2 - t0) * A2;","+        const B2 = (t3 - t) / (t3 - t1) * A2 + (t - t1) / (t3 - t1) * A3;","+        q.push((t2 - t) / (t2 - t1) * B1 + (t - t1) / (t2 - t1) * B2);","+      }","+      out.push([q[0], q[1], q[2], i]);                 // (j === 0: exactly control point i: its knot)","+    }","+  }","+  return out;","+}","+// resample at a fixed spacing ds; returns { L, ds, pts, knots } (knots[i] = s of control point i)","+function resampleClosed(dense, ds) {","+  const n = dense.length, acc = [0], knots = [];","+  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) { const a = dense[i - 1], b = dense[i % n]; acc.push(acc[i - 1] + Math.hypot(b[0] - a[0], b[1] - a[1], b[2] - a[2])); }","+  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (i === 0 || dense[i][3] !== dense[i - 1][3]) knots[dense[i][3]] = acc[i];","+  const L = acc[n], m = Math.round(L / ds), step = L / m, pts = [];","+  for (let k = 0, j = 0; k < m; k++) {","+    const s = k * step; while (acc[j + 1] < s) j++;","+    const a = dense[j], b = dense[(j + 1) % n], f = (s - acc[j]) / (acc[j + 1] - acc[j]);","+    pts.push([a[0] + (b[0] - a[0]) * f, a[1] + (b[1] - a[1]) * f, a[2] + (b[2] - a[2]) * f]);","+  }","+  return { L, ds: step, pts, knots };","+}","+```","+","+**Frame helper (engine axes, left-handed: right = cross(up, forward)):**","+","+```js","+const cross = (a, b) => [a[1] * b[2] - a[2] * b[1], a[2] * b[0] - a[0] * b[2], a[0] * b[1] - a[1] * b[0]];","+const norm = a => { const l = Math.hypot(a[0], a[1], a[2]) || 1; return [a[0] / l, a[1] / l, a[2] / l]; };","+SN.frame = (F, up = [0, 0, 1], bank = 0) => {          // -> { F, R, U }; bank > 0 lowers the right edge","+  const R0 = norm(cross(up, F)), U0 = cross(F, R0), c = Math.cos(bank), s = Math.sin(bank);","+  return { F, R: [R0[0] * c - U0[0] * s, R0[1] * c - U0[1] * s, R0[2] * c - U0[2] * s],","+              U: [U0[0] * c + R0[0] * s, U0[1] * c + R0[1] * s, U0[2] * c + R0[2] * s] };","+};","+// test: SN.frame([1,0,0]).R ≈ [0,1,0] (south = right when heading east); SN.frame([1,0,0],[0,0,1],.3).R[2] < 0 (right edge lower)","+```","+","+**World3D camera pose from a frame:**","+- `yaw = atan2(dir.y, dir.x)` and `pitch = asin(dir.z)` (negative looks down), where `dir` = unit(look − eye);","+- `roll = rollSign · atan2(dot(Ucam, Rflat), dot(Ucam, Zup))`, with `Rflat` = unit(cross(Zup, dir)) under the engine","+  convention.","+","+Set `rollSign` by the M3 screenshot check.","+","+## Appendix B: gibberish voice (`SN.Voice`)","+","+```js","+// say(text, v = { f0, speed, ring, crush, vol }) -> seconds; headless: computes the length, makes no sound","+function say(text, v) {","+  const words = text.toUpperCase().replace(/[^A-Z0-9?!.' ]/g, ' ').split(/\\s+/).filter(Boolean);","+  let t = 0; const ctx = game.audio.ctx, out = sfxBus();            // the engine's effects bus: volume settings apply","+  for (const w of words) {","+    const rnd = E.rng(hashString(w)), n = Math.max(1, Math.round(w.replace(/[^A-Z]/g, '').length / 2.6));","+    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {","+      const vowel = 'AEIOU'['AEIOUY'.indexOf(w[(i * 2 + 1) % w.length]) >= 0 ? 'AEIOU'.indexOf(w[(i * 2 + 1) % w.length]) : (rnd() * 5) | 0];","+      const dur = (0.07 + rnd() * 0.04) / v.speed, last = i === n - 1;","+      const bend = last && /[.]$/.test(w) ? 0.88 : last && /[?]$/.test(w) ? 1.18 : last && /!$/.test(w) ? 1.10 : 1;","+      const f = v.f0 * (0.92 + rnd() * 0.16) * bend;","+      if (ctx) { consonant(ctx, out, t, 2000 + rnd() * 3000, 0.018); vowelTone(ctx, out, t + 0.012, dur, f, FORMANTS[vowel], v); }","+      t += dur + 0.012;","+    }","+    t += 0.06 / v.speed;                                            // a gap between words","+  }","+  return t;","+}","+// FORMANTS = { A: [800, 1200], E: [400, 2000], I: [300, 2300], O: [450, 800], U: [325, 700] }","+// vowelTone: saw osc at f (+ vibrato 6 Hz, 1.5 %) -> two bandpass filters (F1, F2; Q 6) summed -> gain env (8 ms attack,","+// 30 ms release) [-> ring mod (osc * 60 Hz) for ORACLE; -> WaveShaper step curve for KAI-ZERO]; plus 15 % noise.","+```","+","+## Appendix C: planning-script results (`tools/trackcheck.mjs` must reproduce them)","+","+Spline: App. A, k = 48–96, resampled at 2 m (0.5 m for the jumps). Air gravity 40 m/s². Flights start from the lip","+with the lip's slope, 0.9 m above the surface.","+","+| Track | Length | Tightest radius | Slopes (outside jumps) | Overpass | Jump |","+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |","+| Port Solenne | 3.84 km | 56 m (s ≈ 3412, lighthouse) | −5 … +5 % | none | drop cp12→14: an 8.6 m step, 0.65 s air at all speeds; impact 45 m/s −30°, 90 m/s −16°, 170 m/s −6° |","+| Karakum Spire | 4.59 km | 55 m (s ≈ 2408, the spiral) | −8 … +7 % | s 2208 under s 2928: 24.5 m | gap cp7→9, 57 m: 45 m/s hits the wall 20.6 m low, 60 m/s 6.6 m low, **75 m/s clears** (0 m past, −9°), 90 m/s +18 m (−13°), 115 +49 m, 140 +82 m, 170 +129 m (−10°) |","+| Meridian Stack | 4.18 km | 52 m (s ≈ 4167, before the start straight) | −4 … +4 % | s 2082 under s 3983: 31.9 m | drop cp4→6: a 14.2 m step; 45 m/s lands at −37° (hard), 90 m/s −21°, 140 m/s −12°, 170 m/s −11° |","+| Borealis Vault | 5.03 km | 44 m (s ≈ 3774, switchbacks) | −6 … +11 % | none | drop cp6→8: a 17.2 m step; 45 m/s −40° (hard), 90 m/s −23°, 115 m/s −15°, 170 m/s −12° (1.2 s air) |","+","+Lap-time estimates are in §4.2. Hard landings (> 25°) cost energy and speed by design (§3.2 step 5). Slow craft","+are punished, fast ones reward.","+","+## Appendix D: AI Hub session skeleton (`90-hub.js`)","+","+```js","+SN.hub = { active: !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active), info: null, ghosts: new Map(), lastSend: 0, ended: false, reported: false };","+async function startSession() {                               // called from the boot's last step when SN.hub.active","+  const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true });","+  if (!info) { SN.hub.active = false; return game.go('title'); }","+  SN.hub.info = info;","+  const ev = SN.hub.event = { track: ['solenne', 'karakum', 'meridian', 'borealis'][Math.floor(AIHub.rng('event')() * 4)],","+                              cls: 'FLARE', laps: 3, seed: Math.floor(AIHub.rng('race')() * 2 ** 32) };","+  for (const p of info.players) if (p.id !== info.me) SN.hub.ghosts.set(p.id, { id: p.id, name: p.name, color: p.color, s: [] });","+  AIHub.on('card', c => SN.hub.card = c);","+  AIHub.on('start', () => SN.hub.started = true);","+  AIHub.on('message', (d, from) => { const g = SN.hub.ghosts.get(from); if (g && d.t === 'g') g.s.push(Object.assign({ at: AIHub.time() }, d)); if (g && d.t === 'done') g.done = d.ms; });","+  AIHub.on('leave', ({ id }) => SN.hub.ghosts.delete(id));","+  AIHub.on('time-up', () => { if (!SN.hub.reported) { SN.hub.reported = true; AIHub.result({ score: Math.round(myShip().p / 16) }); } });","+  AIHub.on('end', e => { SN.hub.ended = true; SN.hub.endInfo = e; SN.audio.stopAll(); });","+  game.go('loading', { next: 'session', track: ev.track, then: () => AIHub.ready() });   // ready() once the world is warm","+}","+// in the session scene's update: card UI until started; countdown from AIHub.time(); race.step only when time() >= 0 and !ended;","+// every 100 ms: AIHub.send({ t: 'g', p, u, h, y, r, v, lap, fin }, { reliable: false }); every 1000 ms: AIHub.progress({ score, label });","+// on finish: AIHub.result({ time: Math.round(AIHub.time()), stats, awards }); AIHub.send({ t: 'done', ms }); SN.hub.reported = true","+```","+","+## Appendix E: World3D facts learned in planning (saves the builders an afternoon)","+","+- The 3D add-on is an ES module that imports `three/webgpu`, `three/tsl` and `@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat` by bare","+  specifier. It needs an import map. It attaches `E.World3D`, `E.THREE`, `E.TSL` and `E.RAPIER`, and throws if the 2D","+  engine isn't loaded first.","+- `await E.World3D.init(game)` creates one renderer for the page: WebGPU, else WebGL 2 (`?backend=webgl` forces it).","+  It makes `#view` under `#screen` if missing. The game must use `bg: null`. It always initialises Rapier (so Rapier","+  must be loadable even though craft don't use it). Headless it returns at once.","+- `new E.World3D(game, { terrain | level, sky, fog, ambient, sun, lights, glowLights, shadows, filter, camera,","+  onDraw, overlay })`.","+  - `terrain` coordinates run from 0 to `size` (a power-of-two chunk count): keep tracks inside (hence `origin`).","+  - `world.scene` takes your own three.js meshes. Use **node materials only**: MeshLambert / MeshToon / MeshBasic","+    NodeMaterial. Never ShaderMaterial, WebGLRenderer or addons.","+  - Make the materials at world creation. `world.prepare()` builds the shaders. `world.dispose()` before making the","+    next world.","+- The camera has modes `view`, `chase`, `first`, `fly`, `fixed`, `zones` and `behind`. We use `fixed` with","+  `camera.fixed = { x, y, z, yaw, pitch, roll, fov }`. `camera.far` and `camera.near` are in units.","+  `world.addCamera(o)` plus `game.splitScreen(2)` makes a split screen (filters are off while split).","+  `world.project(x, y, z)` gives HUD pixels.","+- `world.step(dt)` in update and `world.draw(r)` in draw, then the HUD with `r.overlay`.","+- `game.particles.*` draw in 3D and land on `particles.ground`. World3D resets that every draw: see edit §2.7.5.","+- Its settings keys: `look3d`, `picture3d` (`pixels` | `balanced` | `full`), `shadows3d`, `filter3d` (`clean` |","+  `cel` | `pixel`). `filter.bloom` is a boolean in the world's `filter`.","+- Its own racing (`world.track`, `world.vehicle`, a Rapier ball) is **not** used. The craft need track-space physics","+  at Wipeout speeds (§3.2), and the tracks need tunnels, drops, gaps and overpasses that `world.track` doesn't model."]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174737__x193f88/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":2187,"oldLines":3,"newStart":2187,"newLines":5,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":7050,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"041395ab-5146-4633-b27b-c7bc1bdf7a40\",\"uuid\":\"a27cf103-576c-4c4c-97e6-e93bed8d81ac\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":7200,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"041395ab-5146-4633-b27b-c7bc1bdf7a40\",\"uuid\":\"df140e74-15a8-4e80-87b7-d0a6df4ce19b\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":7350,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"041395ab-5146-4633-b27b-c7bc1bdf7a40\",\"uuid\":\"10a0a59c-311c-45c5-aa17-ee52ed4bf517\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":7500,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"041395ab-5146-4633-b27b-c7bc1bdf7a40\",\"uuid\":\"f7952bd2-428b-4497-99ab-7418fff4f002\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":7650,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"041395ab-5146-4633-b27b-c7bc1bdf7a40\",\"uuid\":\"4efa8ba7-b094-48cd-b2a5-3e3494aef27b\"}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174749__x8de669/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":2232,"oldLines":3,"newStart":2232,"newLines":4,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cgh2tTBNqwLmQPhHNj6TC\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"It holds only what I created (the two sparse clones and my two scripts, 43 MB). Removing it.\"}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":848,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":547091,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":848},\"output_tokens\":8,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"99d4115e-ac85-4844-bf9c-39009e8f9714\",\"uuid\":\"59003453-69f5-49b1-8d39-b4719feecc07\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:38:25.602Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh2tSumFtgQ67ene57zj\"}"," {\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cgh2tTBNqwLmQPhHNj6TC\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01A7EmBcHQuF1VZkqdRP3C92\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"rm -rf /home/micha/.cache/x8de669-research && ls /home/micha/.cache | grep -c x8de669; ls -la /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174749__x8de669/work\",\"description\":\"Delete my scratch folder and confirm the work folder's contents\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":848,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":547091,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":848},\"output_tokens\":8,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"99d4115e-ac85-4844-bf9c-39009e8f9714\",\"uuid\":\"95a605d1-cd5c-41c0-ad68-8e1a2143af66\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:38:26.766Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh2tSumFtgQ67ene57zj\",\"wire_tool_inputs\":{\"toolu_01A7EmBcHQuF1VZkqdRP3C92\":{\"command\":\"rm -rf /home/micha/.cache/x8de669-research && ls /home/micha/.cache | grep -c x8de669; ls -la /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174749__x8de669/work\",\"description\":\"Delete my scratch folder and confirm the work folder's contents\"}}}"," {\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01A7EmBcHQuF1VZkqdRP3C92\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"0\\ntotal 168\\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 micha micha   4096 Nov  3 18:38 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 micha micha   4096 Nov  3 17:47 ..\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 146137 Nov  3 18:38 PLAN.md\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha    680 Nov  3 17:47 STAGE.md\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha   8772 Nov  3 17:47 TASK.md\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"99d4115e-ac85-4844-bf9c-39009e8f9714\",\"uuid\":\"9bd31372-2820-4628-b499-e3169d50a545\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:38:26.812Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"stdout\":\"0\\ntotal 168\\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 micha micha   4096 Nov  3 18:38 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 micha micha   4096 Nov  3 17:47 ..\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 146137 Nov  3 18:38 PLAN.md\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha    680 Nov  3 17:47 STAGE.md\\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha   8772 Nov  3 17:47 TASK.md\",\"stderr\":\"\",\"interrupted\":false,\"isImage\":false,\"noOutputExpected\":false},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_01A7EmBcHQuF1VZkqdRP3C92\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}","+{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cgh2tgeG9qKKehjnVy554\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"PLAN.md is written in this folder, about 2,200 lines, for builders working from TASK.md and the plan alone.\\n\\n**Title:** *Ahead or Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball*. I brainstormed 50 riffs and drew one with Python's `secrets`, which picked number 27. The game file will be `AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174749.html`.\\n\\n**The game:** six original fighter-women get a fake tournament invitation from jackpot billionaire Sonny Fortuna and his deadpan concierge Pepper. The \\\"tournament\\\" turns out to be a five-day island vacation.\\n- **Matches:** 2-on-2 beach volleyball. One timed button does the bump, set, spike and serve, with a shrinking ring on the sand showing when to press. There are also dives, blocks, shots and \\\"Mine/Yours\\\" calls.\\n- **Partner:** how well your AI partner plays depends on how close you are.\\n- **Days:** each has a morning, afternoon and night. You fill them with matches, gifts and friendships, three shops (21 swimsuits, 10 accessories, 25 gifts), a pool-hopping rhythm game, a casino (slots, blackjack, roulette) and a photo mode.\\n- **Ending:** a final against Sonny & Pepper with special rules, then two comic endings.\\n- **Tone:** campy, like the source, but kept PG.\\n\\n**Camera:** matches use a side-on TV view, with a behind-your-team view as an option. The resort areas use a three-quarter view and the island map is isometric.\\n\\n**What else the plan covers:**\\n- **Architecture:** 33 game modules and 8 small changes to the engine, including PlayStation button symbols in its pixel font, swimsuit drawing on its characters, and a per-letter hook for the gibberish voices.\\n- **Volleyball systems:** an exact formula for the ball's flight, the rules, the timing judge, the AI and its difficulty curve.\\n- **The rest of the TASK.md list:**\\n  - 1–4 local players;\\n  - online play through AI Hub, with its own pre-match screen;\\n  - the attract mode;\\n  - dev mode with 37 tuning sliders and a reset;\\n  - the 10-foot controller UI, with PlayStation symbols by default and Xbox in Settings;\\n  - the touch-only screen;\\n  - all audio made in code;\\n  - the About/Credits page.\\n- **Content:** the cast, every item, a gift-tastes table, schedules, story beats, the tutorial script, and a writing guide with sample lines.\\n- **Build order:** 11 milestones, volleyball first, each ending playable.\\n- **Risks:** 13 of them, each with a way to de-risk it.\\n- **Testing:** one `npm test` command running 19 suites, including a full scripted playthrough and the AI Hub's own test harness; screenshots and animation strips only for the art.\\n\\n**Research:** I read the engine's docs and sports kit, the AI Hub protocol and its test harness, and the engine's motion-capture catalog, so the plan uses their real calls. I did no web research on the source game itself; it's described from general knowledge.\\n\\n**For the builder:** `/tmp` on this machine was 98% full during planning, so the plan tells the builder to point npm and Playwright at a temp folder inside the dev folder if they hit \\\"no space\\\" errors. I deleted my own scratch clones afterwards.\"}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":463,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":547939,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":463},\"output_tokens\":781,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"99d4115e-ac85-4844-bf9c-39009e8f9714\",\"uuid\":\"34319b20-a67c-47b7-856f-0cd9562d2b52\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:38:38.431Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh2tgMtx4ZAMbAtna7eP\"}"]}]}],"moreFiles":7,"changedFiles":["/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183312__xf5fb84/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174728__x978410/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174728__x978410/work/PLAN.md","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174737__x193f88/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174749__x8de669/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174752__xfb4127/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174807__x5c5240/work/PLAN.md","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174810__xbd1c73/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174822__x21985c/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174831__x898ed5/agent.out","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-182910__x9a2bbb/work/IceHoc-Kaboom-IceHockey-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-182910/src/10-synth.js","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183213__xf5fb84/agent.out"]}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TbQZHLyHmTWuApC1YvwMwp","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
