{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016CqjEbAF61iCNR7fbrg3RC","type":"tool_result","content":"26:## 0. Quick facts\n40:## 1. Ground rules for the builder\n59:   Engine Feedback. The patches this plan needs are in §15.6.\n66:## 2. What makes Smash TV distinctive, and how MASH TV makes it play\n86:## 3. The game at a glance\n107:## 4. Controls and input\n109:### 4.1 Bindings\n127:### 4.2 Engine input presets\n148:- **Preset sync hook (engine patch P1, §15.6).** While the pause menu (the Shell) or one of our overlays is open in play,\n157:### 4.3 How a contestant reads its intent each step\n177:### 4.4 Prompts and glyphs\n198:## 5. Systems and mechanics\n203:### 5.1 Contestants (the players)\n248:### 5.2 Weapons and power-ups\n274:### 5.3 Pickups and economy\n304:### 5.4 Ratings (the show's heartbeat)\n330:### 5.5 Arena, doors and hazards\n377:### 5.6 Room flow (phases)\n401:### 5.7 Waves: the room script data format\n429:### 5.8 Enemies\n468:### 5.9 Bosses\n564:### 5.10 Scoring, lives, continue, high scores\n581:### 5.11 Difficulty and player scaling\n587:### 5.12 The broadcast layer (how the show talks)\n604:## 6. Content overview\n619:### 6.1 Every episode uses the same map shape\n646:## 7. Episode and room content\n648:### 7.1 EPISODE 1: THE PILOT (Studio 9, set `studio`)\n718:### 7.2 EPISODE 2: SWEEPS WEEK (the MegaMart Prize Warehouse, set `warehouse`)\n778:### 7.3 EPISODE 3: THE SEASON FINALE (the Rooftop Gala, set `rooftop`, at night)\n841:### 7.4 THE GREEN ROOM (bonus, set `greenroom`)\n853:### 7.5 THE LIGHTNING ROUND (AI Hub sessions only)\n862:### 7.6 Prizes (§5.3) and Penny's joke for each\n896:## 8. Story beats and script\n908:### 8.1 Cold Open (after the lobby)\n925:### 8.2 Episode beats\n953:### 8.3 Enemy introductions (the first appearance in a run doubles as the tutorial)\n967:### 8.4 Line pools (random without repeats; rate-limited by §5.12)\n1010:## 9. Presentation: art direction, assets, animation, effects\n1012:### 9.1 Art direction\n1035:### 9.2 Sets (one per episode, plus the Green Room)\n1051:### 9.3 Characters\n1075:### 9.4 New art assets to author (list them in `src/06-art-studio.js`, `07-art-chars.js` and `08-sprites.js`)\n1119:### 9.5 Animation (the engine's system as the base, plus new motion where the source needs it)\n1160:### 9.6 Effects, lighting, feel\n1174:## 10. Audio (everything synthesized in code)\n1176:### 10.1 Music (engine notation songs in `src/03-audio-music.js`, all original)\n1198:### 10.2 Sound effects (`audio.define` in `src/04-audio-sfx.js`)\n1211:### 10.3 Babble: Animalese/Simlish voices (`src/05-babble.js`)\n1258:## 11. UI and UX (10-foot, controller-first)\n1260:### 11.1 Rules for every screen\n1275:### 11.2 The UI kit (`src/30-ui-kit.js`)\n1285:### 11.3 Screens\n1307:### 11.4 The in-play HUD (drawn in `r.overlay`, anchored to `r.W` and `r.H`)\n1329:### 11.5 Pause menu and settings\n1360:### 11.6 Keyboard and mouse feel native\n1367:### 11.7 Touch-only devices\n1379:## 12. Attract mode\n1420:## 13. Dev mode (pause menu → DEV MODE; standalone only, never in hub sessions or the attract mode)\n1422:### 13.1 Panel\n1437:### 13.2 Tabs\n1487:## 14. Multiplayer\n1489:### 14.1 Local co-op (the game on its own)\n1502:### 14.2 AI Hub sessions (Session Protocol v1, `shared-world`)\n1570:### 14.3 Netcode: who simulates what\n1638:### 14.4 The round's end and standings\n1651:### 14.5 Fallback (only if shared-world can't be made to pass)\n1662:## 15. Architecture\n1664:### 15.1 Folder layout (the dev folder)\n1683:### 15.2 Modules (`src/`; each file is a fragment of one IIFE that `00-core` opens and `90-main` closes)\n1719:### 15.3 Data model and frame order\n1764:### 15.4 Scenes, deep links and the debug handle\n1786:### 15.5 Engine patches (each marked in the source and listed in `engine/PATCHES.md`; they feed the Engine Feedback)\n1795:**§15.6** is the same list, under the name §1 and §4 point to. Patch only what the game can't do itself: CSS\n1799:### 15.7 Coding rules\n1812:## 16. Build and packaging\n1843:## 17. Testing\n1845:### 17.1 The one command\n1859:### 17.2 Suites: what each checks\n1884:### 17.3 What only eyes and ears can judge\n1896:## 18. Build order and milestones\n1923:## 19. Risks and how to de-risk them\n1942:## 20. Finish checklist\n1980:## Appendix A. Research behind this plan","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17","uuid":"b6db55b1-63ef-4351-8cd1-8a6c15439f6c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:43:56.678Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"26:## 0. Quick facts\n40:## 1. Ground rules for the builder\n59:   Engine Feedback. The patches this plan needs are in §15.6.\n66:## 2. What makes Smash TV distinctive, and how MASH TV makes it play\n86:## 3. The game at a glance\n107:## 4. Controls and input\n109:### 4.1 Bindings\n127:### 4.2 Engine input presets\n148:- **Preset sync hook (engine patch P1, §15.6).** While the pause menu (the Shell) or one of our overlays is open in play,\n157:### 4.3 How a contestant reads its intent each step\n177:### 4.4 Prompts and glyphs\n198:## 5. Systems and mechanics\n203:### 5.1 Contestants (the players)\n248:### 5.2 Weapons and power-ups\n274:### 5.3 Pickups and economy\n304:### 5.4 Ratings (the show's heartbeat)\n330:### 5.5 Arena, doors and hazards\n377:### 5.6 Room flow (phases)\n401:### 5.7 Waves: the room script data format\n429:### 5.8 Enemies\n468:### 5.9 Bosses\n564:### 5.10 Scoring, lives, continue, high scores\n581:### 5.11 Difficulty and player scaling\n587:### 5.12 The broadcast layer (how the show talks)\n604:## 6. Content overview\n619:### 6.1 Every episode uses the same map shape\n646:## 7. Episode and room content\n648:### 7.1 EPISODE 1: THE PILOT (Studio 9, set `studio`)\n718:### 7.2 EPISODE 2: SWEEPS WEEK (the MegaMart Prize Warehouse, set `warehouse`)\n778:### 7.3 EPISODE 3: THE SEASON FINALE (the Rooftop Gala, set `rooftop`, at night)\n841:### 7.4 THE GREEN ROOM (bonus, set `greenroom`)\n853:### 7.5