{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh3KfFsBNTEarFbsjVMk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XNbmCM8LVLDBvw24b338cR","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174807__x5c5240/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":707,"cache_read_input_tokens":473022,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":707},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17","uuid":"008116b1-606f-48cd-a45e-d82a68972e9b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:44:17.597Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh3KeyzWBBwyyYgpWaP9","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01XNbmCM8LVLDBvw24b338cR":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174807__x5c5240/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":200}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XNbmCM8LVLDBvw24b338cR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# MASH TV\n2\t\n3\t*A playful riff on Smash TV. I brainstormed fifty riffs on the source's name and numbered them, then drew one with Python's\n4\t`secrets.randbelow(50) + 1`. It came up 9: **MASH TV**. Don't re-pick the title.*\n5\t\n6\t**Pitch.** MASH TV is a 1–4 player twin-stick arena shooter set inside the deadliest game show on television. Contestants\n7\tfight through single-screen studio rooms while goons, robots and worse pour in through four doors. They grab cash and\n8\tridiculous prizes, play to the cameras to keep the **ratings** up, and pick a route through the studio map. The goal is to\n9\tsurvive three episodes, each capped by a giant boss, and reach the Season Finale. Collect enough Golden Keys and the\n10\tsecret **Green Room** opens at the end. Your host is the immortal, oily, sequinned **Lance Lustre**: *\"KA-CHING! KA-BOOM!\"*\n11\t\n12\t**Names and files** (use the model id and timestamp from *your* TASK.md):\n13\t\n14\t| What | Value |\n15\t|---|---|\n16\t| Title on screen | `MASH TV` (sub-line: `LIVE FROM STUDIO 9`) |\n17\t| GameTitle in file names | `MashTV` |\n18\t| SOURCE in file names | `SmashTV` |\n19\t| Dev folder (all dev files) | `MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<timestamp>/` |\n20\t| Deliverable (root, beside TASK.md) | `MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<timestamp>.html` |\n21\t| Exit interview (root, beside the HTML) | `exit-interview.json` |\n22\t| \"Plan\" field in Source Information | `PLAN.md` |\n23\t\n24\t---\n25\t\n26\t## 0. Quick facts\n27\t\n28\t| | |\n29\t|---|---|\n30\t| Genre | Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade). Co-op for 1–4 players, with a route choice between rooms. |\n31\t| Perspective | One fixed single-screen arena per room, seen from a high three-quarter \"studio camera\": the engine view `threequarter` (pitch 55). `topdown` (pitch 80) is a Settings option. |\n32\t| Engine | my-3D2dge handoff package **v0.85.2** (`github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge`, `dist/handoff/`; commit `4390a98` when planned). Use the full edition, `my-3d2dge.js`, which includes every module (we use `arcade`). |\n33\t| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client `aihub-session 1.4.0`, **mode `shared-world`**, 1–4 players. |\n34\t| Local multiplayer | 1–4 players on one screen: P1 on keyboard+mouse or a pad, P2–P4 on pads (drop-in with Options/Start). |\n35\t| Length | A full run takes about 20–25 minutes: three episodes of 2–4 rooms plus a boss, then the bonus Green Room. A hub session round takes about 5–6 minutes. |\n36\t| Output | One self-contained HTML file with the engine, the hub client and the game inlined. No image or audio files; everything is drawn and synthesized in code. |\n37\t\n38\t---\n39\t\n40\t## 1. Ground rules for the builder\n41\t\n42\t1. **TASK.md is the contract; this plan is how to meet it.** Where they disagree, TASK.md wins.\n43\t2. **Work only inside your dev folder.** Other agents share the parent directories, ports and processes. Read nothing\n44\t   outside your folder except what you fetch:\n45\t   - Engine: shallow-clone it **inside your dev folder** (e.g. `.cache/my-3d2dge`; when this plan was written, `/tmp` on this\n46\t     machine was a full tmpfs) with `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge .cache/my-3d2dge`.\n47\t     Copy `dist/handoff/*` into the dev folder: the engine, the docs, the tools, the kits and `src/assets`.\n48\t   - Hub: **don't clone ai-hub** (it's over 1 GB). Fetch the four files you need:\n49\t     `curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/{session.js,harness.html,bridge.js,round.js}`\n50\t     into `vendor/hub/`. `lib/hub/README.md` is the protocol; read it once, whole.\n51\t3. **Servers and processes.** Prefer `file://` and headless Node. The hub harness needs a server: bind `127.0.0.1` on port 0\n52\t   (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints the port, or a Node `http` server on port 0). Before trusting a\n53\t   result, check that the page is your game (`window.MT` exists and the title is `MASH TV`). Stop only processes you\n54\t   started, by PID. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`.\n55\t4. **Read the engine docs before writing code.** Read `API.md` whole. Read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before drawing props.\n56\t   Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names; read the `AI_GUIDE.md` sections on Standard features, Frame order, Players, Testing\n57\t   and the Twin-stick recipe. Every API name in this plan was checked against v0.85.2. If your copy is newer, grep the index.\n58\t5. **Engine patches are allowed but kept small**, each listed with its reason in `engine/PATCHES.md`. That list feeds the\n59\t   Engine Feedback. The patches this plan needs are in §15.5.\n60\t6. **No TODOs, placeholders, stubs or \"coming soon\" anywhere.** Every menu item works; every feature in this plan ships.\n61\t7. **One test command** (`npm test` → `node tools/test.mjs`) prints only failures and a one-line count. Rerun only the\n62\t   suites whose code you changed (`--only <suite>`), and run everything before you hand off.\n63\t\n64\t---\n65\t\n66\t## 2. What makes Smash TV distinctive, and how MASH TV makes it play\n67\t\n68\t| Source trait | MASH TV mechanic or feature |\n69\t|---|---|\n70\t| A lethal TV game show played as entertainment, with a smarmy host | **Lance Lustre** reacts to everything on a **jumbotron** above the arena, with a lower-third subtitle and gibberish voice. The **Ratings** system turns \"be entertaining\" into a mechanic: it drives a cash multiplier, Prime Time, and producer \"stunts\" when you dawdle. |\n71\t| Hordes flooding in through four doors | Door-spawn wave director: each door opens, enemies pour through and the door slams shut. Up to ~60 enemies on screen. |\n72\t| Two-stick 8-way shooting while moving | Analog twin-stick (pad), mouse aim (PC), and 8-way arrow-key fire (keyboard only). |\n73\t| Power-ups, cash and absurd consumer prizes | Timed weapons, cash × the ratings multiplier, and named joke prizes (\"A SELF-AWARE TOASTER\"). Co-host **Penny Prize** tallies them in a Prize Recap after each episode. |\n74\t| A room-to-room map with path choice | Episode maps (3×3 grid). After a clear, the exits light up with the destination's name; walk through one to choose. Longer paths hold keys and cash; shorter paths carry less risk. |\n75\t| Giant, multi-part bosses | **Mashy** the mascot (shoot off its parts), the **Wheel of Misfortune** (each spin picks the attack), **Lance in the Starmaker 9000**, then **The Network** itself. |\n76\t| A secret bonus for collectors | **Golden Keys** (9 in the game). With 5 or more after the finale, you reach the **Green Room** and the true ending. |\n77\t| Simultaneous co-op | 1–4 players in one arena, locally or online (AI Hub shared world). Ratings and keys are shared; cash is individual. |\n78\t\n79\t**Tone:** loud, garish and cartoon-violent. Enemies burst into **confetti, sparks and smoke** (no blood: \"BY ORDER OF THE\n80\tFCC, ALL GORE HAS BEEN REPLACED WITH CONFETTI!\"). It's consumerist satire with a retro-future TV aesthetic: neon, chrome,\n81\tmarquee bulbs, sponsor logos. **Writing style:** short, shouted, ALL-CAPS announcer lines with punchlines and fake sponsor\n82\tplugs, like the source's barker energy. Every line is original (§8.6).\n83\t\n84\t---\n85\t\n86\t## 3. The game at a glance\n87\t\n88\t**Core loop (one sentence):** enter a sealed studio room, survive the waves pouring from its doors while grabbing cash,\n89\tprizes and power-ups, then pick the next door on the studio map, until you reach and beat the episode's boss.\n90\t\n91\t**Flow:**\n92\t\n93\t```\n94\tBoot ─► Title ─(10 s idle)─► Attract demo ─(any input)─► Title\n95\tTitle menu: START THE SHOW · EPISODES · HOW TO PLAY · TOP EARNERS · OPTIONS · ABOUT & CREDITS\n96\tSTART THE SHOW ─► Difficulty ─► Contestant Lobby (join 1–4) ─► Cold Open ─► Episode 1 card\n97\t  ─► Room ─► (clear) ─► choose exit ─► \"NEXT ON MASH TV\" map card ─► Room … ─► Boss ─► Prize Recap\n98\t  ─► Episode 2 card … Boss ─► Prize Recap ─► Episode 3 card … Finale (Lance ─► The Network) ─► Prize Recap\n99\t  ─► keys ≥ 5 ? Green Room ─► Ending B (true ending) : Ending A\n100\t  ─► Initials (each qualifying player) ─► Top Earners ─► Title\n101\tAll lives lost (every player out) ─► CONTINUE? 10…0 (✕/Enter: continue, −50 % winnings) ─► else Game Over ─► Initials ─► Top Earners ─► Title\n102\tAI Hub session: Our own card (contestant line-up) ─► 3-2-1 ─► Lightning Round (3 rooms + Mashy) ─► finish ─► end card\n103\t```\n104\t\n105\t---\n106\t\n107\t## 4. Controls and input\n108\t\n109\t### 4.1 Bindings\n110\t\n111\t| Action | DualSense (default glyphs) | Xbox glyphs (Settings) | Keyboard + mouse |\n112\t|---|---|---|---|\n113\t| Move | L-stick, D-pad | L-stick, D-pad | W A S D |\n114\t| Aim + fire | R-stick (fires while pushed past 0.35) | R-stick | Mouse aims (reticle); **hold Left click** fires |\n115\t| Fire (in the last aim direction) | R2 (hold) | RT | — |\n116\t| 8-way fire (keyboard only) | — | — | Arrow keys (hold; two arrows = diagonal) |\n117\t| Slide (dodge with i-frames) | L2, or ✕ | LT, or A | Space or Shift |\n118\t| Showstopper (screen bomb) | R1, or ○ | RB, or B | E or Right click |\n119\t| Showboat (taunt for ratings) | L1, or △ | LB, or Y | Q |\n120\t| Pause / menu | Options | Menu | Esc or P |\n121\t| Menus: move / confirm / back | D-pad, L-stick / ✕ / ○ | D-pad / A / B | Arrows, WASD / Enter, Space / Esc, Backspace; mouse hover focuses, click picks, wheel scrolls |\n122\t\n123\tThe face buttons are alternates for players who don't use the right stick. While twin-sticking, the thumbs are on the\n124\tsticks, so every combat action is on a shoulder or trigger. All actions can be rebound on the engine's standard Controls\n125\tscreen.\n126\t\n127\t### 4.2 Engine input presets\n128\t\n129\tDefine two presets and switch between them. Arrows must **aim** in play but **navigate** in menus. The engine keeps\n130\tarrows on the moves only if a preset lists them, so the play preset leaves them out.\n131\t\n132\t```js\n133\t// A preset lists every action it uses, the menu's own (start, pause, confirm, cancel) included.\n134\t// Mouse clicks in menus go through input.clicked() on the hovered item, not through 'confirm'.\n135\tconst MASH_MENU = { up: ['KeyW','ArrowUp','Pad12'], down: ['KeyS','ArrowDown','Pad13'], left: ['KeyA','ArrowLeft','Pad14'],\n136\t  right: ['KeyD','ArrowRight','Pad15'], start: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad9'], pause: ['Escape','KeyP','Pad9'],\n137\t  confirm: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Space','Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape','Backspace','Pad1'],\n138\t  tabL: ['KeyQ','Pad4'], tabR: ['KeyE','Pad5'], reset: ['KeyR','Pad3'] };          // tabs/reset: dev mode & pages\n139\tconst MASH_PLAY = { up: ['KeyW','Pad12'], down: ['KeyS','Pad13'], left: ['KeyA','Pad14'], right: ['KeyD','Pad15'],\n140\t  aimUp: ['ArrowUp'], aimDown: ['ArrowDown'], aimLeft: ['ArrowLeft'], aimRight: ['ArrowRight'],\n141\t  fire: ['Mouse0','Pad7'], slide: ['Space','ShiftLeft','ShiftRight','Pad6','Pad0'],\n142\t  bomb: ['KeyE','Mouse2','Pad5','Pad1'], showboat: ['KeyQ','Pad4','Pad3'],\n143\t  start: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad9'], pause: ['Escape','KeyP','Pad9'],\n144\t  confirm: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape','Backspace','Pad1'] };\n145\t```\n146\t\n147\t- Set `input: MASH_MENU` on front-end scenes and `input: MASH_PLAY` on play scenes (the scene's `input` field).\n148\t- **Preset sync hook (engine patch P1, §15.5).** While the pause menu (the Shell) or one of our overlays is open in play,\n149\t  every player's input must use `MASH_MENU`, so arrows move the focus. Expose the engine's internal `HOOKS` as `E.HOOKS`\n150\t  and push an input hook. The hook runs after `input.tick` and before the Shell reads navigation. It calls\n151\t  `p.use(want)` on each of `game.players` when the wanted preset changes: `MENU` if `game.paused || MT.overlay.open`,\n152\t  else the scene's preset. Changing presets calls `clear()`, which is fine at those moments.\n153\t- Joined players (`game.addPlayer`) copy P1's preset when they join. The hook keeps them in sync afterwards.\n154\t- Name the actions on the Controls screen with `menu.controls`: `{ fire: 'Fire', slide: 'Slide', bomb: 'Showstopper',\n155\t  showboat: 'Showboat', aimUp: 'Fire up (keyboard)', … , tabL: false, tabR: false, reset: false }`.\n156\t\n157\t### 4.3 How a contestant reads its intent each step\n158\t\n159\t`intentFrom(input, contestant)` returns `{ move:[x,y], aim: angle|null, fire: bool, slide: bool(pressed), bomb:\n160\tbool(pressed), showboat: bool(pressed) }`:\n161\t\n162\t1. **Move:** `input.move()` is in screen directions. Convert it with `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`.\n163\t2. **Aim**, in priority order:\n164\t   - The right stick, `input.padAim` (non-null past the dead zone; length > 0.35). Aim = the stick's screen direction\n165\t     converted to ground. Fire is true if Auto-fire is on (the default).\n166\t   - Arrow keys held: an 8-way direction from `aimUp/Down/Left/Right`. Fire is true.\n167\t   - The mouse, when `input.lastDevice === 'mouse'` or the mouse moved within the last 2 s. Aim = the angle from the\n168\t     contestant to `game.mouseGround()`. Fire = `down('fire')` (Left click).\n169\t   - Otherwise keep the last aim. `down('fire')` (R2) fires along it; if there's no aim yet, use the facing.\n170\t3. **Aim assist** (Settings, on by default for pads only): if an enemy is within 170 units and within ±10° of the aim,\n171\t   rotate the aim halfway toward it.\n172\t4. **Facing** follows the aim while firing; otherwise it follows movement.\n173\t\n174\tBots (attract mode, tests, the dev \"bot partner\") and remote players (§14) produce the same intent object. A contestant\n175\tnever reads an `E.Input` directly.\n176\t\n177\t### 4.4 Prompts and glyphs\n178\t\n179\t- Prompts follow `input.lastDevice`. On `gamepad`, show the button glyph in the family from the setting `glyphs`. On\n180\t  `keyboard`/`mouse`, show the keycap or mouse icon for that device's binding. Find the code with\n181\t  `input.map[action]` (first `Pad…` for pads; first key or `Mouse…` for keyboard and mouse); `input.codeLabel(code)`\n182\t  gives the text.\n183\t- **The 5×7 font has no ✕ ○ □ △.** Build `src/02-glyphs.js`: small pixel icons, 11×11 at scale 1, drawn with `E.px`.\n184\t  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#7aa8ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8` and △ green `#5fe0a0`, each on a dark disc. L1, R1,\n185\t    L2 and R2 are rounded tabs. Options and Create are pills; the sticks are L/R ringed discs; plus a D-pad cross.\n186\t  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue and Y yellow letter discs; LB, RB, LT, RT, Menu and View.\n187\t  - Keyboard: a keycap with the label (1–6 chars; wide caps for Space, Shift, Enter and Esc).\n188\t  - Mouse: a mouse body with the left or right half lit.\n189\t\n190\t  API: `MT.glyph.draw(g, x, y, action, input, { scale })` returns the width it drew;\n191\t  `MT.glyph.prompt(g, x, y, [[action, 'TEXT'], …], input)` draws a row of icons with labels.\n192\t- **Engine patch P2:** the standard `glyphs` setting defaults to `'playstation'`, its options are\n193\t  `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox']]`, and its `apply` sets `glyphs` on **every** `game.players` input. A\n194\t  player who joins later inherits it.\n195\t\n196\t---\n197\t\n198\t## 5. Systems and mechanics\n199\t\n200\tAll numbers below are defaults. Every one marked **(T)** is a live tuning value with a dev slider (§13). Logic always reads"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17","uuid":"f46182af-2964-461b-988c-f735fe270a78","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:44:17.602Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174807__x5c5240/work/PLAN.md","content":"# MASH TV\n\n*A playful riff on Smash TV. I brainstormed fifty riffs on the source's name and numbered them, then drew one with Python's\n`secrets.randbelow(50) + 1`. It came up 9: **MASH TV**. Don't re-pick the title.*\n\n**Pitch.** MASH TV is a 1–4 player twin-stick arena shooter set inside the deadliest game show on television. Contestants\nfight through single-screen studio rooms while goons, robots and worse pour in through four doors. They grab cash and\nridiculous prizes, play to the cameras to keep the **ratings** up, and pick a route through the studio map. The goal is to\nsurvive three episodes, each capped by a giant boss, and reach the Season Finale. Collect enough Golden Keys and the\nsecret **Green Room** opens at the end. Your host is the immortal, oily, sequinned **Lance Lustre**: *\"KA-CHING! KA-BOOM!\"*\n\n**Names and files** (use the model id and timestamp from *your* TASK.md):\n\n| What | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Title on screen | `MASH TV` (sub-line: `LIVE FROM STUDIO 9`) |\n| GameTitle in file names | `MashTV` |\n| SOURCE in file names | `SmashTV` |\n| Dev folder (all dev files) | `MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<timestamp>/` |\n| Deliverable (root, beside TASK.md) | `MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<timestamp>.html` |\n| Exit interview (root, beside the HTML) | `exit-interview.json` |\n| \"Plan\" field in Source Information | `PLAN.md` |\n\n---\n\n## 0. Quick facts\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Genre | Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade). Co-op for 1–4 players, with a route choice between rooms. |\n| Perspective | One fixed single-screen arena per room, seen from a high three-quarter \"studio camera\": the engine view `threequarter` (pitch 55). `topdown` (pitch 80) is a Settings option. |\n| Engine | my-3D2dge handoff package **v0.85.2** (`github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge`, `dist/handoff/`; commit `4390a98` when planned). Use the full edition, `my-3d2dge.js`, which includes every module (we use `arcade`). |\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client `aihub-session 1.4.0`, **mode `shared-world`**, 1–4 players. |\n| Local multiplayer | 1–4 players on one screen: P1 on keyboard+mouse or a pad, P2–P4 on pads (drop-in with Options/Start). |\n| Length | A full run takes about 20–25 minutes: three episodes of 2–4 rooms plus a boss, then the bonus Green Room. A hub session round takes about 5–6 minutes. |\n| Output | One self-contained HTML file with the engine, the hub client and the game inlined. No image or audio files; everything is drawn and synthesized in code. |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Ground rules for the builder\n\n1. **TASK.md is the contract; this plan is how to meet it.** Where they disagree, TASK.md wins.\n2. **Work only inside your dev folder.** Other agents share the parent directories, ports and processes. Read nothing\n   outside your folder except what you fetch:\n   - Engine: shallow-clone it **inside your dev folder** (e.g. `.cache/my-3d2dge`; when this plan was written, `/tmp` on this\n     machine was a full tmpfs) with `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge .cache/my-3d2dge`.\n     Copy `dist/handoff/*` into the dev folder: the engine, the docs, the tools, the kits and `src/assets`.\n   - Hub: **don't clone ai-hub** (it's over 1 GB). Fetch the four files you need:\n     `curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/{session.js,harness.html,bridge.js,round.js}`\n     into `vendor/hub/`. `lib/hub/README.md` is the protocol; read it once, whole.\n3. **Servers and processes.** Prefer `file://` and headless Node. The hub harness needs a server: bind `127.0.0.1` on port 0\n   (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints the port, or a Node `http` server on port 0). Before trusting a\n   result, check that the page is your game (`window.MT` exists and the title is `MASH TV`). Stop only processes you\n   started, by PID. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`.\n4. **Read the engine docs before writing code.** Read `API.md` whole. Read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before drawing props.\n   Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names; read the `AI_GUIDE.md` sections on Standard features, Frame order, Players, Testing\n   and the Twin-stick recipe. Every API name in this plan was checked against v0.85.2. If your copy is newer, grep the index.\n5. **Engine patches are allowed but kept small**, each listed with its reason in `engine/PATCHES.md`. That list feeds the\n   Engine Feedback. The patches this plan needs are in §15.5.\n6. **No TODOs, placeholders, stubs or \"coming soon\" anywhere.** Every menu item works; every feature in this plan ships.\n7. **One test command** (`npm test` → `node tools/test.mjs`) prints only failures and a one-line count. Rerun only the\n   suites whose code you changed (`--only <suite>`), and run everything before you hand off.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What makes Smash TV distinctive, and how MASH TV makes it play\n\n| Source trait | MASH TV mechanic or feature |\n|---|---|\n| A lethal TV game show played as entertainment, with a smarmy host | **Lance Lustre** reacts to everything on a **jumbotron** above the arena, with a lower-third subtitle and gibberish voice. The **Ratings** system turns \"be entertaining\" into a mechanic: it drives a cash multiplier, Prime Time, and producer \"stunts\" when you dawdle. |\n| Hordes flooding in through four doors | Door-spawn wave director: each door opens, enemies pour through and the door slams shut. Up to ~60 enemies on screen. |\n| Two-stick 8-way shooting while moving | Analog twin-stick (pad), mouse aim (PC), and 8-way arrow-key fire (keyboard only). |\n| Power-ups, cash and absurd consumer prizes | Timed weapons, cash × the ratings multiplier, and named joke prizes (\"A SELF-AWARE TOASTER\"). Co-host **Penny Prize** tallies them in a Prize Recap after each episode. |\n| A room-to-room map with path choice | Episode maps (3×3 grid). After a clear, the exits light up with the destination's name; walk through one to choose. Longer paths hold keys and cash; shorter paths carry less risk. |\n| Giant, multi-part bosses | **Mashy** the mascot (shoot off its parts), the **Wheel of Misfortune** (each spin picks the attack), **Lance in the Starmaker 9000**, then **The Network** itself. |\n| A secret bonus for collectors | **Golden Keys** (9 in the game). With 5 or more after the finale, you reach the **Green Room** and the true ending. |\n| Simultaneous co-op | 1–4 players in one arena, locally or online (AI Hub shared world). Ratings and keys are shared; cash is individual. |\n\n**Tone:** loud, garish and cartoon-violent. Enemies burst into **confetti, sparks and smoke** (no blood: \"BY ORDER OF THE\nFCC, ALL GORE HAS BEEN REPLACED WITH CONFETTI!\"). It's consumerist satire with a retro-future TV aesthetic: neon, chrome,\nmarquee bulbs, sponsor logos. **Writing style:** short, shouted, ALL-CAPS announcer lines with punchlines and fake sponsor\nplugs, like the source's barker energy. Every line is original (§8.6).\n\n---\n\n## 3. The game at a glance\n\n**Core loop (one sentence):** enter a sealed studio room, survive the waves pouring from its doors while grabbing cash,\nprizes and power-ups, then pick the next door on the studio map, until you reach and beat the episode's boss.\n\n**Flow:**\n\n```\nBoot ─► Title ─(10 s idle)─► Attract demo ─(any input)─► Title\nTitle menu: START THE SHOW · EPISODES · HOW TO PLAY · TOP EARNERS · OPTIONS · ABOUT & CREDITS\nSTART THE SHOW ─► Difficulty ─► Contestant Lobby (join 1–4) ─► Cold Open ─► Episode 1 card\n  ─► Room ─► (clear) ─► choose exit ─► \"NEXT ON MASH TV\" map card ─► Room … ─► Boss ─► Prize Recap\n  ─► Episode 2 card … Boss ─► Prize Recap ─► Episode 3 card … Finale (Lance ─► The Network) ─► Prize Recap\n  ─► keys ≥ 5 ? Green Room ─► Ending B (true ending) : Ending A\n  ─► Initials (each qualifying player) ─► Top Earners ─► Title\nAll lives lost (every player out) ─► CONTINUE? 10…0 (✕/Enter: continue, −50 % winnings) ─► else Game Over ─► Initials ─► Top Earners ─► Title\nAI Hub session: Our own card (contestant line-up) ─► 3-2-1 ─► Lightning Round (3 rooms + Mashy) ─► finish ─► end card\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Controls and input\n\n### 4.1 Bindings\n\n| Action | DualSense (default glyphs) | Xbox glyphs (Settings) | Keyboard + mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Move | L-stick, D-pad | L-stick, D-pad | W A S D |\n| Aim + fire | R-stick (fires while pushed past 0.35) | R-stick | Mouse aims (reticle); **hold Left click** fires |\n| Fire (in the last aim direction) | R2 (hold) | RT | — |\n| 8-way fire (keyboard only) | — | — | Arrow keys (hold; two arrows = diagonal) |\n| Slide (dodge with i-frames) | L2, or ✕ | LT, or A | Space or Shift |\n| Showstopper (screen bomb) | R1, or ○ | RB, or B | E or Right click |\n| Showboat (taunt for ratings) | L1, or △ | LB, or Y | Q |\n| Pause / menu | Options | Menu | Esc or P |\n| Menus: move / confirm / back | D-pad, L-stick / ✕ / ○ | D-pad / A / B | Arrows, WASD / Enter, Space / Esc, Backspace; mouse hover focuses, click picks, wheel scrolls |\n\nThe face buttons are alternates for players who don't use the right stick. While twin-sticking, the thumbs are on the\nsticks, so every combat action is on a shoulder or trigger. All actions can be rebound on the engine's standard Controls\nscreen.\n\n### 4.2 Engine input presets\n\nDefine two presets and switch between them. Arrows must **aim** in play but **navigate** in menus. The engine keeps\narrows on the moves only if a preset lists them, so the play preset leaves them out.\n\n```js\n// A preset lists every action it uses, the menu's own (start, pause, confirm, cancel) included.\n// Mouse clicks in menus go through input.clicked() on the hovered item, not through 'confirm'.\nconst MASH_MENU = { up: ['KeyW','ArrowUp','Pad12'], down: ['KeyS','ArrowDown','Pad13'], left: ['KeyA','ArrowLeft','Pad14'],\n  right: ['KeyD','ArrowRight','Pad15'], start: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad9'], pause: ['Escape','KeyP','Pad9'],\n  confirm: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Space','Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape','Backspace','Pad1'],\n  tabL: ['KeyQ','Pad4'], tabR: ['KeyE','Pad5'], reset: ['KeyR','Pad3'] };          // tabs/reset: dev mode & pages\nconst MASH_PLAY = { up: ['KeyW','Pad12'], down: ['KeyS','Pad13'], left: ['KeyA','Pad14'], right: ['KeyD','Pad15'],\n  aimUp: ['ArrowUp'], aimDown: ['ArrowDown'], aimLeft: ['ArrowLeft'], aimRight: ['ArrowRight'],\n  fire: ['Mouse0','Pad7'], slide: ['Space','ShiftLeft','ShiftRight','Pad6','Pad0'],\n  bomb: ['KeyE','Mouse2','Pad5','Pad1'], showboat: ['KeyQ','Pad4','Pad3'],\n  start: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad9'], pause: ['Escape','KeyP','Pad9'],\n  confirm: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape','Backspace','Pad1'] };\n```\n\n- Set `input: MASH_MENU` on front-end scenes and `input: MASH_PLAY` on play scenes (the scene's `input` field).\n- **Preset sync hook (engine patch P1, §15.5).** While the pause menu (the Shell) or one of our overlays is open in play,\n  every player's input must use `MASH_MENU`, so arrows move the focus. Expose the engine's internal `HOOKS` as `E.HOOKS`\n  and push an input hook. The hook runs after `input.tick` and before the Shell reads navigation. It calls\n  `p.use(want)` on each of `game.players` when the wanted preset changes: `MENU` if `game.paused || MT.overlay.open`,\n  else the scene's preset. Changing presets calls `clear()`, which is fine at those moments.\n- Joined players (`game.addPlayer`) copy P1's preset when they join. The hook keeps them in sync afterwards.\n- Name the actions on the Controls screen with `menu.controls`: `{ fire: 'Fire', slide: 'Slide', bomb: 'Showstopper',\n  showboat: 'Showboat', aimUp: 'Fire up (keyboard)', … , tabL: false, tabR: false, reset: false }`.\n\n### 4.3 How a contestant reads its intent each step\n\n`intentFrom(input, contestant)` returns `{ move:[x,y], aim: angle|null, fire: bool, slide: bool(pressed), bomb:\nbool(pressed), showboat: bool(pressed) }`:\n\n1. **Move:** `input.move()` is in screen directions. Convert it with `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`.\n2. **Aim**, in priority order:\n   - The right stick, `input.padAim` (non-null past the dead zone; length > 0.35). Aim = the stick's screen direction\n     converted to ground. Fire is true if Auto-fire is on (the default).\n   - Arrow keys held: an 8-way direction from `aimUp/Down/Left/Right`. Fire is true.\n   - The mouse, when `input.lastDevice === 'mouse'` or the mouse moved within the last 2 s. Aim = the angle from the\n     contestant to `game.mouseGround()`. Fire = `down('fire')` (Left click).\n   - Otherwise keep the last aim. `down('fire')` (R2) fires along it; if there's no aim yet, use the facing.\n3. **Aim assist** (Settings, on by default for pads only): if an enemy is within 170 units and within ±10° of the aim,\n   rotate the aim halfway toward it.\n4. **Facing** follows the aim while firing; otherwise it follows movement.\n\nBots (attract mode, tests, the dev \"bot partner\") and remote players (§14) produce the same intent object. A contestant\nnever reads an `E.Input` directly.\n\n### 4.4 Prompts and glyphs\n\n- Prompts follow `input.lastDevice`. On `gamepad`, show the button glyph in the family from the setting `glyphs`. On\n  `keyboard`/`mouse`, show the keycap or mouse icon for that device's binding. Find the code with\n  `input.map[action]` (first `Pad…` for pads; first key or `Mouse…` for keyboard and mouse); `input.codeLabel(code)`\n  gives the text.\n- **The 5×7 font has no ✕ ○ □ △.** Build `src/02-glyphs.js`: small pixel icons, 11×11 at scale 1, drawn with `E.px`.\n  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#7aa8ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8` and △ green `#5fe0a0`, each on a dark disc. L1, R1,\n    L2 and R2 are rounded tabs. Options and Create are pills; the sticks are L/R ringed discs; plus a D-pad cross.\n  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue and Y yellow letter discs; LB, RB, LT, RT, Menu and View.\n  - Keyboard: a keycap with the label (1–6 chars; wide caps for Space, Shift, Enter and Esc).\n  - Mouse: a mouse body with the left or right half lit.\n\n  API: `MT.glyph.draw(g, x, y, action, input, { scale })` returns the width it drew;\n  `MT.glyph.prompt(g, x, y, [[action, 'TEXT'], …], input)` draws a row of icons with labels.\n- **Engine patch P2:** the standard `glyphs` setting defaults to `'playstation'`, its options are\n  `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox']]`, and its `apply` sets `glyphs` on **every** `game.players` input. A\n  player who joins later inherits it.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Systems and mechanics\n\nAll numbers below are defaults. Every one marked **(T)** is a live tuning value with a dev slider (§13). Logic always reads","numLines":200,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1995}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XNbmCM8LVLDBvw24b338cR","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
